QB.add(85,[{"n":1,"q":"A male patient presents to the emergency department he is dyspneic, a known case of COPD. The arterial blood gas report is as follows: pH, 7.28; pCO2, 60 mmHg; and HCO3, 28meq/L. which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Inadequately compensated respiratory acidosis"},{"t":"Fully compensated respiratory acidosis"},{"t":"Fully compensated respiratory alkalosis"},{"t":"Inadequately compensated metabolic alkalosis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Inadequately compensated respiratory acidosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"pH 7.28 is decreased, suggestive of acidosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"pCO2 = 60mmHg, since it is elevated, the acidosis is likely respiratory."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The bicarbonate is 28 meq/L implying that compensation is partial (partial compensation/inadequate compensation)"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Fully compensated respiratory acidosis - This option is incorrect. In a patient with fully compensated respiratory acidosis, the pH is expected to be within the normal range, unlike in this scenario."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Fully compensated respiratory alkalosis - This option is incorrect since this patient is currently acidotic."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Inadequate metabolic alkalosis - This option is incorrect since this patient has respiratory acidosis."}]},{"n":2,"q":"Comment on the lung disease?","o":[{"t":"Hypersensitivity pneumonitis"},{"t":"Sarcoidosis"},{"t":"Acute respiratory distress syndrome"},{"t":"Bronchiectasis"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Bronchiectasis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The given flow volume loop represents an obstructive lung disease, and among the given options, bronchiectasis is the only obstructive lung disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Obstructive lung diseases are characterized by obstruction or narrowing of the airways, which makes it difficult for air to flow out of the lungs during exhalation. This results in diminished expiratory flows, meaning that it takes longer for the lungs to empty than normal. (The Expiratory curve shows a scooped-out appearance.)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Additionally, due to the air trapping caused by the obstruction, lung volumes, particularly the residual volume (RV) and total lung capacity (TLC), increase, resulting in the shift of the curve to the left (larger volumes)."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options: Options A, B, and C are restrictive lung diseases, and the flow volume shifts to the right in restrictive lung diseases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In restrictive lung diseases, the lungs have decreased compliance, which limits their ability to expand fully during inhalation. As a result, lung volumes (decreased vital capacity) are smaller during each respiratory cycle."}],"qi":[10259]},{"n":3,"q":"Patient was taking ampicillin for URTI. Then he started having watery diarrhea for the past 3 days. Drug of choice to manage this condition?","o":[{"t":"Metronidazole"},{"t":"Fidaxomicin"},{"t":"Ciprofloxacin"},{"t":"Bezlotoxumab"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B – Fidaxomicin:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The diagnosis is pseudomembranous colitis. The drug of choice in this patient is fidaxomicin/vancomycin."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A – Metronidazole: Metronidazole can be used for severe diarrhea associated with C. difficile but it is not the drug of choice. Metronidazole is used only if vancomycin or fidaxomicin is not readily accessible."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C – Ciprofloxacin: Fluoroquinolones such as ciprofloxacin can potentially cause pseudomembranous colitis,"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D – Bezlotoxumab: It is a Monoclonal antibody used in recurrent pseudomembranous colitis. It is not the first-line treatment in this condition."}]},{"n":4,"q":"A 70-year-old diabetic man presents after a fall. He is having a high fever, cough and breathing difficulty and appears confused. CXR shows lobar consolidation. Labs show elevated BUN and creatinine. Which is the most appropriate management?","o":[{"t":"Inpatient management with levofloxacin."},{"t":"Inpatient management with ceftriaxone."},{"t":"Discharge home on amoxicillin with clavulanic acid."},{"t":"Discharge home on azithromycin."}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Inpatient management with levofloxacin:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Parameters to be considered for the CURB -65 score in this case are: age, elevated BUN, and presence of confusion."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The total score is 3 which indicates that the patient needs hospitalization."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Since the patient is also diabetic, he needs to be started on of the following: Respiratory Fluoroquinolones Amoxicillin + Clavulanic acid and Azithromycin Cephalosporin + Azithromycin Doxycycline"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Respiratory Fluoroquinolones"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Amoxicillin + Clavulanic acid and Azithromycin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cephalosporin + Azithromycin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Doxycycline"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Inpatient management with ceftriaxone : This option is incorrect since the patient cannot be treated with a cephalosporin alone."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Discharge home on amoxicillin with clavulanic acid : This option is incorrect since the patient needs to be treated on an inpatient basis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Discharge home on azithromycin : This option is incorrect since the patient needs to be treated on an inpatient basis."}]},{"n":5,"q":"Patient was being treated in ICU for sepsis ABG shows pH=6.9, pO2 =80 mm Hg. pCO2=55 mm Hg, HCO3 = 10 meq/L. Repeat ABG after 2 hour shows pH=6.7, pCO2= 20 mm Hg, pO2 = 100 mm Hg. HCO3 = 5 meq/L. Which is most plausible to explain the change seen in ABG report?","o":[{"t":"Excessive air and less heparin in ABG sample"},{"t":"Excess air and more heparin in ABG sample"},{"t":"Less air and more heparin in ABG sample"},{"t":"Less air and less heparin in ABG sample"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Excess air and more heparin in ABG sample:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The ABG values are suggestive of Metabolic acidosis with respiratory acidosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Heparin is a sulfated mucopolysaccharide with acidic properties. An excess of heparin in the ABG syringe can have the following effects"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Effect on pH"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"If the pH is normal or alkaline, to begin with- Acidemia increases -pH will fall on contact with the acidic heparin ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"If the pH is very acidic, to begin with- Academia decreases - pH will rise as the mildly acidic heparin reduces the greater acidity of the blood."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Excessive air and less heparin in ABG sample: This option is incorrect"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Less air and more heparin in ABG sample: This option is incorrect"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Less air and less heparin in ABG sample: This option is incorrect"}]},{"n":6,"q":"A 33-year-old woman is being evaluated for daytime sleepiness and memory loss which have been present for the past 1 year. Her BMI is 43, her BP is 170/98 mmHg. In an awake state, ABG shows pO2 70 mmHg, pCO2 53 mmHg, HCO3 33 mmol/L. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome"},{"t":"Central Sleep Apnea"},{"t":"Narcolepsy"},{"t":"Obstructive sleep Apnea"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome (OHS):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This disorder occurs in severely obese individuals who experience hypoventilation (inadequate breathing) during sleep and wakefulness. The elevated BMI, daytime sleepiness, and memory loss are consistent with OHS. The elevated pCO2 (53 mmHg) and elevated HCO3 (33 mmol/L) on the ABG indicate chronic respiratory acidosis and compensation, which is a characteristic feature of OHS. The high blood pressure (BP) also supports this diagnosis as OHS is associated with increased cardiovascular risks."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In summary given the patient's severe obesity, daytime sleepiness, memory loss, high BMI, elevated BP, and the ABG results showing chronic respiratory acidosis, the most likely diagnosis is Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Central Sleep Apnea (CSA): CSA involves a dysfunction in the central nervous system's control of breathing during sleep, leading to temporary cessation of breathing. While CSA can cause daytime sleepiness, the ABG values (elevated pCO2 and HCO3) suggest a respiratory-related issue rather than a primary central nervous system problem."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Narcolepsy: Narcolepsy is a neurological disorder characterized by excessive daytime sleepiness, often accompanied by sudden muscle weakness (cataplexy), sleep paralysis, and vivid dream-like hallucinations. It doesn't typically involve significant respiratory disturbances as seen in this case."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Obstructive Sleep Apnea (OSA): OSA is a condition where the airway becomes partially or completely blocked during sleep, leading to repeated pauses in breathing. While OSA can cause daytime sleepiness and memory issues, the ABG findings of elevated pCO2 and HCO3 are more indicative of a chronic respiratory problem like OHS."}]},{"n":7,"q":"A 60-year-old bedridden patient develops sudden onset breathlessness and chest pain. CTPA was done. Which of the following non-invasive investigations will be useful to confirm the etiology of this presentation?","o":[{"t":"D dimer"},{"t":"MRI"},{"t":"Coagulogram"},{"t":"Doppler"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"D - Doppler: The sudden onset of breathlessness and chest pain in a bedridden patient, along with the performance of a CTPA, raises suspicion for a pulmonary embolism. Doppler scan will identify deep vein thrombosis and is the best non-invasive investigation test to confirm the etiology of Pulmonary embolism"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - D dimer is a marker of fibrin degradation and is elevated in conditions associated with clot formation and breakdown, such as deep vein thrombosis (DVT) and pulmonary embolism (PE). In this case, the patient's sudden onset of breathlessness and chest pain raises suspicion for a possible pulmonary embolism. A CTPA (computed tomography pulmonary angiography) was performed, a standard imaging technique to diagnose PE. If the CTPA shows evidence of a clot in the pulmonary arteries, a high D dimer level could support the diagnosis, as it indicates ongoing clot formation and breakdown. However it is not the best non invasive investigation ."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - MRI: Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) is a valuable imaging tool for various medical conditions, but it might not be the most suitable choice in this scenario. CTPA is the preferred imaging modality for assessing pulmonary embolism due to its ability to provide detailed images of the pulmonary vasculature. While MRI could be useful in certain cases, such as evaluating cardiac function or soft tissue structures, it's not the primary investigation for confirming PE."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Coagulogram: A coagulogram is a comprehensive test that evaluates various aspects of blood clotting and can help diagnose bleeding disorders or abnormalities in the clotting cascade. While it might provide some insight into the patient's coagulation status, it wouldn't be the most direct investigation to confirm the cause of sudden onset breathlessness and chest pain, which is suggestive of a possible pulmonary embolism."}],"qi":[10263]},{"n":8,"q":"In a patient with HIV, the incorrect statement regarding prophylaxis for various organisms is: PCP-Pneumocystis pneumonia MAC-Mycobacterium avium complex","o":[{"t":"Prophylaxis for PCP is indicated when CD4 count is <200 cells/µL"},{"t":"Stop prophylaxis for Coccidioides if CD4 count is >250 cells/µL for 6 months"},{"t":"Prophylaxis for MAC is indicated when CD4 count is <50 cells /µL"},{"t":"Prophylaxis for cryptococcus is indicated when CD4 count is >150 cells/µL"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Prophylaxis for cryptococcus is indicated when CD4 count is >150 cells/µL:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prophylaxis for cryptococcus is indicated when CD4 count is >150 cells/µL : This statement is incorrect. Prophylaxis for cryptococcal meningitis is indicated in HIV patients with a CD4 count below 100 cells/µL, not >150 cells/µL. Cryptococcus is an opportunistic fungal infection commonly seen in individuals with advanced HIV disease."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Prophylaxis for PCP is indicated when CD4 count is <200 cells/µL: This statement is correct. Prophylaxis for Pneumocystis pneumonia (PCP) is recommended in HIV patients with a CD4 count below 200 cells/µL to prevent the development of opportunistic infections."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Stop prophylaxis for Coccidioides if CD4 count is >250 cells/µL for 6 months: This statement is correct. Prophylaxis for Coccidioides (a fungal infection) can be discontinued in HIV patients who have a sustained increase in CD4 count above 250 cells/µL for at least 6 months and have been on antiretroviral therapy."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Prophylaxis for MAC is indicated when CD4 count is <50 cells/µL: This statement is correct. Prophylaxis for Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) is recommended in HIV patients with a CD4 count below 50 cells/µL to prevent disseminated MAC disease."}]},{"n":9,"q":"A 45-year-old woman with obstructive lung disease is currently using salmeterol and tiotropium but continues to experience breathlessness and frequent exacerbations. Given her current situation, which of the following treatments would be most appropriate?","o":[{"t":"Nebulization with LAMA+LABA"},{"t":"Long-Term Oxygen Therapy (LTOT)"},{"t":"Positive Pressure Ventilation (PPV)"},{"t":"Pneumococcal Vaccine"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Long-Term Oxygen Therapy (LTOT):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Long-term oxygen therapy is the best choice for this patient as it addresses potential hypoxemia and can help manage symptoms and reduce exacerbations in chronic obstructive lung disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The other options either provide symptomatic relief or are preventive measures but do not directly address the core issue of ongoing breathlessness and frequent exacerbations."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Nebulization with LAMA+LABA: Combining a Long-Acting Muscarinic Antagonist (LAMA) with a Long-Acting Beta-Agonist (LABA) in nebulized form may offer additional symptom relief, but this approach might not be sufficient for severe cases or for addressing the underlyingissues leading to frequent exacerbations."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Positive Pressure Ventilation (PPV): PPV can be beneficial in acute respiratory failure or severe cases of COPD exacerbation. It helps by aiding the breathing process through mechanical support. However, it is generally considered when other interventions fail and the patient is in a more critical state."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Pneumococcal Vaccine: The pneumococcal vaccine can help prevent infections that could exacerbate respiratory conditions. While important for overall lung health, it would not directly address the patient's current issues with breathlessness and exacerbations."}]},{"n":10,"q":"A patient presents with wheezing that improves with as-needed use of albuterol. Spirometry shows FEV1 ranging from 70% to 83%, and the patient experiences nighttime chest tightening twice a week. What is the most appropriate treatment?","o":[{"t":"Continue with albuterol"},{"t":"Replace with salmeterol twice daily"},{"t":"Start Tab prednisolone"},{"t":"Add an inhaled corticosteroid"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Add an inhaled corticosteroid:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Asthma Classification: Based on the patient's symptoms and spirometry results, the patient is likely experiencing persistent mild asthma . The FEV1 ranging from 70% to 83% suggests some airflow limitation, and nighttime symptoms occurring twice a week indicate inadequate control of asthma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Based on the patient's symptoms and spirometry results, the patient is likely experiencing persistent mild asthma . The FEV1 ranging from 70% to 83% suggests some airflow limitation, and nighttime symptoms occurring twice a week indicate inadequate control of asthma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Management: Inhaled corticosteroids (ICS) are the mainstay of treatment for persistent asthma. They reduce inflammation and help prevent exacerbations. Given the frequency of nighttime symptoms, it is necessary to step up the treatment from just using a short-acting beta-agonist (like albuterol) as needed."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Inhaled corticosteroids (ICS) are the mainstay of treatment for persistent asthma. They reduce inflammation and help prevent exacerbations. Given the frequency of nighttime symptoms, it is necessary to step up the treatment from just using a short-acting beta-agonist (like albuterol) as needed."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Asthma Classification:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Based on the patient's symptoms and spirometry results, the patient is likely experiencing persistent mild asthma . The FEV1 ranging from 70% to 83% suggests some airflow limitation, and nighttime symptoms occurring twice a week indicate inadequate control ofasthma."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Based on the patient's symptoms and spirometry results, the patient is likely experiencing persistent mild asthma . The FEV1 ranging from 70% to 83% suggests some airflow limitation, and nighttime symptoms occurring twice a week indicate inadequate control of asthma."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Inhaled corticosteroids (ICS) are the mainstay of treatment for persistent asthma. They reduce inflammation and help prevent exacerbations. Given the frequency of nighttime symptoms, it is necessary to step up the treatment from just using a short-acting beta-agonist (like albuterol) as needed."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Inhaled corticosteroids (ICS) are the mainstay of treatment for persistent asthma. They reduce inflammation and help prevent exacerbations. Given the frequency of nighttime symptoms, it is necessary to step up the treatment from just using a short-acting beta-agonist (like albuterol) as needed."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Continue with albuterol : This is insufficient for managing persistent asthma. Albuterol is a short-acting bronchodilator used for quick relief, not for long-term control."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Replace with salmeterol twice daily : Salmeterol is a long-acting beta-agonist (LABA) and should not be used alone without an ICS because it does not address the underlying inflammation and can increase the risk of asthma-related death."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Start Tab prednisolone : Oral corticosteroids like prednisolone are reserved for acute exacerbations or severe persistent asthma. They are not recommended for routine management due to potential side effects."}]},{"n":11,"q":"Patient presents with recurrent lung infections and CXR is given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Kartagener syndrome"},{"t":"Cystic fibrosis"},{"t":"Digeorge syndrome"},{"t":"Down syndrome"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Kartagener syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Kartagener syndrome is characterized by a triad of symptoms: situs inversus (dextrocardia seen on CXR), chronic sinusitis, and bronchiectasis. The recurrent lung infections in this case are consistent with the chronic respiratory issues seen in Kartagener syndrome. The dextrocardia noted in the CXR supports this diagnosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Cystic fibrosis: Cystic fibrosis primarily presents with thick, sticky mucus leading to lung infections, pancreatic insufficiency, and other issues. While it causes recurrent lung infections, it is not associated with dextrocardia. The diagnosis is typically supported by sweat chloride tests and genetic testing."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - DiGeorge syndrome: DiGeorge syndrome includes a range of congenital defects, including heart defects, thymic hypoplasia, and hypoparathyroidism. It does not typically present with dextrocardia or recurrent lung infections as a primary feature."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Down syndrome: Down syndrome is associated with a variety of congenital heart defects and may include respiratory issues. However, it is not specifically associated with dextrocardia or a particular pattern of recurrent lung infections that would align with Kartagener syndrome."}],"qi":[10271]},{"n":12,"q":"What is the expected finding in a patient with the following CXR?","o":[{"t":"Kussmaul breathing"},{"t":"Paradoxical breathing"},{"t":"Apneustic breathing"},{"t":"Bronchial breathing"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Paradoxical breathing:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Flail chest is a condition characterized by the fracture of multiple consecutive ribs in at least two places, creating a free-floating segment of the chest wall. This results in paradoxical breathing, where the flail segment moves inward during inspiration (instead of expanding) and outward during expiration (instead of contracting). This abnormal movement is due to the loss of structural integrity in the chest wall."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Kussmaul breathing: Kussmaul breathing is deep and labored breathing associated with metabolic acidosis, particularly diabetic ketoacidosis. It is not related to flail chest or broken ribs."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Apneustic breathing: Apneustic breathing is characterized by prolonged inspiratory phases with short expiratory phases and is typically seen in brainstem injuries. It is unrelated to the mechanical consequences of flail chest."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Bronchial breathing: Bronchial breathing refers to harsh, hollow breath sounds heard in areas of lung consolidation, such as in pneumonia. It is not associated with flail chest, which primarily affects the mechanics of breathing rather than lung sounds."}],"qi":[10277]},{"n":13,"q":"A patient presents with acute epigastric pain and an increase in serum lipase levels. Three days after stabilization, a chest X-ray is obtained. What is the most common pathology behind the chest X-ray findings?","o":[{"t":"Increased PCWP"},{"t":"Pulmonary thromboembolism"},{"t":"Non cardiogenic pulmonary edema"},{"t":"Aspiration pneumonitis"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In the context of acute pancreatitis, which is indicated by the elevated serum lipase levels and acute epigastric pain, the most common chest X-ray finding could be related to non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema. This is often a complication of acute pancreatitis due to the release of inflammatory mediators that can lead to increased capillary permeability in the lungs, causing pulmonary edema."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The colonic cut-off sign mentioned is a radiographic finding associated with acute pancreatitis, where there is a gas-filled segment of the colon that abruptly ends at the level of the pancreatic inflammation, usually at the splenic flexure. While this sign itself is more directly related to the abdominal pathology, the systemic inflammatory response in severe pancreatitis can lead to complications such as non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema, reflected in the chest X-ray."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Increased PCWP (Pulmonary Capillary Wedge Pressure): This finding is typically associated with cardiogenic pulmonary edema, often due to heart failure. In pancreatitis, the pulmonary edema is usually non-cardiogenic, with normal or low PCWP."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Pulmonary thromboembolism: While pulmonary embolism can present with acute chest pain and respiratory symptoms, it is not typically associated with acute pancreatitis. There would also be other clinical signs, like sudden onset of dyspnea, that are not mentioned in this case."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Aspiration pneumonitis: This condition occurs due to the inhalation of gastric contents, leading to lung inflammation. It usually presents with respiratory symptoms like coughing and wheezing shortly after the event. There’s no indication in the case of an aspiration event, and it is less likely related to pancreatitis."}],"qi":[10283]},{"n":14,"q":"Most common cause of death in idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis?","o":[{"t":"Respiratory failure"},{"t":"Pulmonary edema"},{"t":"Cancer"},{"t":"PAH"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Respiratory failure:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis (IPF) is a chronic, progressive lung disease characterized by the gradual scarring of lung tissue, which leads to the deterioration of lung function. The most common cause of death in patients with IPF is respiratory failure. As the disease progresses, the lung tissue becomes increasingly fibrotic, reducing its ability to transfer oxygen into the bloodstream and remove carbon dioxide from it, leading to severe respiratory insufficiency."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Pulmonary edema: Pulmonary edema refers to fluid accumulation in the alveoli and interstitial spaces of the lungs, often due to heart failure or other conditions. While it can occur in various pulmonary conditions, it is not the most common cause of death in IPF."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Cancer: While patients with IPF may have an increased risk of developing lung cancer, cancer is not the most common cause of death in IPF. The primary cause of mortality remains respiratory failure due to progressive lung fibrosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - PAH (Pulmonary Arterial Hypertension): Pulmonary Arterial Hypertension (PAH) can occur as a complication of IPF, but it is not the most common cause of death. PAH can contribute to respiratory failure but is not the primary cause of mortality in IPF."}]},{"n":15,"q":"A patient with a history of pneumonia develops pleural effusion. What is the expected finding in the pleural analysis for parapneumonic effusion?","o":[{"t":"Pleural protein/plasma protein ratio less than 0.5"},{"t":"Pleural LDH less than 2/3rd of plasma LDH"},{"t":"Pleural pH less than 7.2"},{"t":"Pleural LDH less than 0.6 of plasma LDH"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"A pleural pH of less than 7.2 is a key diagnostic indicator of complicated parapneumonic effusion, an exudative pleural effusion associated with pneumonia. This acidic pH develops due to increased glucose metabolism by bacteria and inflammatory cells in the pleural space, resulting in lactic acid accumulation. A low pleural fluid pH is an important indication for chest tube drainage."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Exudative Effusion: Meets at least one of the following criteria"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Transudative Effusion: Meets none of the criteria Parapneumonic effusion"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Definition: A parapneumonic effusion is a"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"pleural effusion that occurs in association with bacterial pneumonia, lung abscess, orbronchiectasis. These effusions develop in about 40-60% of patients with bacterial pneumonia."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Types: Parapneumonic effusions can be"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"categorized into three stages: simple (uncomplicated), complicated, and empyema. Simple parapneumonic effusions , the pleural fluid is sterile and can resolve with antibiotic therapy alone. Complicated parapneumonic effusions contain bacteria and require drainage in addition to antibiotics. Empyema represents the most advanced stage where frank pus is present in the pleural space."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Diagnostic Criteria: Simple Parapneumonic Effusion Appearance: Serous"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"PMN++, sterile fluid (negative bacterial culture) pH >7.3, LDH <500 U/L, glucose >80 mg/dL Fluid-glucose: serum-glucose >0.5"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Complicated Appearance: Turbid Parapneumonic PMN++, bacterial culture may be + Effusion or -"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"pH <7.1, LDH >1000 U/L, glucose <40 mg/dL Fluid-glucose: serum-glucose <0.5"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Empyema Appearance: Pus"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"PMN++, bacterial culture positive or negative pH <7.1, LDH >1000 U/L, glucose <40 mg/dL Fluid-glucose: serum-glucose <0.5"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Management: Drain with antibiotics; small-bore"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"drains (10-14 gauge) are often preferable. Consider thoracoscopic decortication if these fail."}]},{"n":16,"q":"A 62-year-old woman with a history of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) presents with increased breathlessness, cough, and sputum production over the last two days. She appears distressed and has a respiratory rate of 28 breaths per minute. Her oxygen saturation is 88% on room air. Which of the following management strategies is most appropriate for her immediate treatment?","o":[{"t":"Initiate high-flow oxygen therapy to maintain oxygen saturation above 95%"},{"t":"Start nebulised bronchodilator therapy with salbutamol and ipratropium"},{"t":"Begin intravenous glucocorticoids immediately"},{"t":"Order a chest X-ray before any treatment is provided"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Start nebulised bronchodilator therapy with salbutamol and ipratropium"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nebulised bronchodilator therapy with salbutamol and ipratropium is the most appropriate immediate treatment for this patient experiencing an acute exacerbation of COPD."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This therapy helps relieve bronchospasm and improve airflow, which is critical given her increased breathlessness and distress."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment of Acute Exacerbation of COPD:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Initial Management and Assessment:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms: Increased breathlessness, cough, sputum production; deterioration in lung function and health status."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Triggers: Usually bacterial or viral infections, or changes in air quality."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Home management: Increased bronchodilator therapy, short course of oral glucocorticoids, and possibly antibiotics."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hospital referral indicators: Cyanosis Peripheral oedema Altered consciousness"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cyanosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Peripheral oedema"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Altered consciousness"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Comprehensive assessment upon admission: Level of consciousness (response to commands, ability to cough) Signs of CO2 retention (warm peripheries, bounding pulse, flapping tremor) Airway obstruction assessment (wheeze, prolonged expiration) Signs of cor pulmonale (peripheral oedema, raised JVP) Background functional status and quality of life Precipitating causes (e.g., infection, environmental factors)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Level of consciousness (response to commands, ability to cough)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Signs of CO2 retention (warm peripheries, bounding pulse, flapping tremor)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Airway obstruction assessment (wheeze, prolonged expiration)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Signs of cor pulmonale (peripheral oedema, raised JVP)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Background functional status and quality of life"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Precipitating causes (e.g., infection, environmental factors)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cyanosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Peripheral oedema"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Altered consciousness"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Level of consciousness (response to commands, ability to cough)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Signs of CO2 retention (warm peripheries, bounding pulse, flapping tremor)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Airway obstruction assessment (wheeze, prolonged expiration)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Signs of cor pulmonale (peripheral oedema, raised JVP)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Background functional status and quality of life"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Precipitating causes (e.g., infection, environmental factors)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Investigation Purpose/Findings"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Arterial blood Assess severity of hypoxaemia, hypercapnia, gases acidaemia; guide oxygen therapy and ventilation."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Chest X-ray Identify complications such as pneumonia,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"pneumothorax, or pleural effusion. (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management Strategies:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Management Details"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Oxygen Therapy Administer controlled oxygen at 24% or 28% to maintain"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"PaO2 > 8 kPa (60 mmHg) or SaO2 > 90%. (Option A ruled out) Regular arterial blood gas monitoring to adjust therapy."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Bronchodilators Use nebulised short-acting b2-agonists (e.g.,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"salbutamol, terbutaline) with anticholinergics (e.g., ipratropium). If oxygen sensitivity is a concern, use compressed air for nebulisation."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Glucocorticoids Administer oral prednisolone (30 mg for 10 days) to"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"reduce symptoms and improve lung function.Reduces frequency and severity of exacerbations. Consider osteoporosis prophylaxis for repeated courses."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Antibiotic Therapy Indicated for increased sputum purulence, volume, or"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"breathlessness. Use simple regimens (aminopenicillin, tetracycline, macrolide); co-amoxiclav in areas with b-lactamase organisms."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Non-Invasive Safe for mild to moderate respiratory acidosis; helps Ventilation (NIV) reduce intubation need and mortality."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Not suitable for patients unable to protect their airway."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Mechanical Ventilation Consider if there is a reversible cause, no prior"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"respiratory failure, or if NIV has failed/contraindicated. Decisions should involve the patient, family, and GP."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Additional Therapy Diuretics for peripheral oedema."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Limited evidence for intravenous aminophylline; consider arrhythmia risk. Doxapram may be used short-term for low respiratory rates."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Discharge and Follow-Up:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Discharge criteria: Clinically stable on maintenance medication. Off nebulised therapy for at least 24 hours. PEF reaches 75% of predicted or personal best."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinically stable on maintenance medication."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Off nebulised therapy for at least 24 hours."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"PEF reaches 75% of predicted or personal best."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Post-exacerbation follow-up: Identify and avoid trigger factors. Provide asthma education and a written self-management plan."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Identify and avoid trigger factors."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Provide asthma education and a written self-management plan."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinically stable on maintenance medication."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Off nebulised therapy for at least 24 hours."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"PEF reaches 75% of predicted or personal best."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Identify and avoid trigger factors."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Provide asthma education and a written self-management plan."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Begin intravenous glucocorticoids immediately (Option C) is not the first-line treatment in the acute setting; while glucocorticoids are important in managing exacerbations, nebulised bronchodilators are prioritised for immediate relief of symptoms."}]},{"n":17,"q":"A 35-year-old female patient presents to you with fever, breathlessness, and cough with expectoration. A CT scan was done which is shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Consolidation with air bronchogram"},{"t":"Mediastinal mass"},{"t":"Pleural effusion"},{"t":"Diaphragmatic hernia"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Consolidation with air bronchogram:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Consolidation with air bronchogram refers to a radiological finding on chest imaging, where a locale of lung parenchyma shows up more opaque due to accumulation of fluid or inflammatory exudate, with unmistakable air-filled bronchi inside it."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Causes can be bacterial or viral pneumonia, bronchiectasis, pulmonary hemorrhage, or other inflammatory lung diseases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The CT scan reveals consolidation with an air bronchogram, which is a classic finding in pneumonia. The patient moreover presents with fever, breathlessness, and cough with expectoration, which are indications of pneumonia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options :"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Mediastinal mass: A mediastinal mass is an anomalous development or tumor found within the mediastinum, the area between the lungs within the chest. The CT scan would show a well-defined mass within the mediastinum. The side effects would depend on the size of the mass."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Pleural effusion: Pleural effusion is a buildup of liquid between the layers of tissue that line the lungs and chest wall. The CT scan would appear as a collection of fluid around the lungs."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Diaphragmatic hernia: A diaphragmatic hernia may be a defect within the diaphragm that permits organs from the abdomen to move into the chest depth. The CT scan would clearly demonstrate the presence of abdominal organs in the chest."}],"qi":[10290]},{"n":18,"q":"Which drugs are ineffective against multidrug-resistant (MDR) tuberculosis?","o":[{"t":"Cycloserine ·"},{"t":"Fluoroquinolones"},{"t":"Isoniazid and rifampicin"},{"t":"Kanamycin"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Isoniazid and rifampicin:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Multidrug-resistant (MDR) tuberculosis shows resistance to isoniazid and rifampicin, which are the two most effective first-line anti-TB drugs."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options A: Cycloserine:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MOA: Inhibits alanine racemase and ligase"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Oral drug for multidrug resistant TB"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bacteriostatic agent"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Side effects: Highly neurotoxic drug Psychosis Depression: Increased risk for suicides"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Psychosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Depression: Increased risk for suicides"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Psychosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Depression: Increased risk for suicides"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B- Fluoroquinolone : Fluoroquinolones are second-line anti-TB drugs that are used to treat MDR-TB together with different drugs. MDR-TB does show resistance to isoniazid and rifampicin but not to fluoroquinolones."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Kanamycin: Kanamycin is a second-line anti-TB drug used in the treatment of MDR-TB. MDR-TB does show resistance to isoniazid and rifampicin but not to kanamycin."}]},{"n":19,"q":"A male patient with a history of chronic smoking presents with the complaint of dysphagia. Out of the following, which type of lung cancer is most likely to be found in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Squamous cell carcinoma"},{"t":"Adenocarcinoma"},{"t":"Large cell carcinoma"},{"t":"Bronchogenic carcinoma"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Squamous cell carcinoma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dysphagia can be caused by various conditions, including cancer of the esophagus or lung. However, lung cancer is more commonly associated with dysphagia when it involves the upper part of the lung or the lymph nodes around the trachea, which can press on the esophagus and interfere with swallowing. Given that the patient in question has a history of chronic smoking, squamous cell carcinoma is the most likely type of lung cancer to be found. This is because squamous cell carcinoma is strongly associated with smoking and tends to develop in the central part of the lung, near the bronchus."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":20,"q":"A patient presents with chronic complaints of a productive cough in the morning. The expectoration is yellow, foul smelling and increases on turning from left to right side. Which of the following lung diseases is likely to be present?","o":[{"t":"Lung cancer"},{"t":"Bronchiectasis"},{"t":"Bronchial asthma"},{"t":"Pulmonary embolism"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Bronchiectasis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bronchiectasis is a chronic respiratory condition characterized by permanent dilation and thickening of the bronchial walls, leading to impaired airway mucus clearance. This can result in persistent coughing, often accompanied by sputum production, which can worsen on changing body position."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Here, the cough increases on turning from left to right side, known as the \"dependent lung\" phenomenon. This occurs because when the patient turns onto the affected side, the mucus collects in the dependent part of the lung and triggers a cough."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":21,"q":"What is the diagnosis based on the image given below?","o":[{"t":"Pulmonary tuberculosis"},{"t":"Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease"},{"t":"Bronchial asthma"},{"t":"Bronchiectasis"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Bronchiectasis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bronchiectasis is a condition characterized by permanent and abnormal dilatation of the bronchi and bronchioles, which results in impaired airway clearance and chronic infections. Imaging studies can confirm the diagnosis of bronchiectasis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The CT findings in bronchiectasis may include: Bronchial dilatation: The most important finding in bronchiectasis is bronchial dilatation, which may be cylindrical or varicose in shape. The dilatation may be segmental or involve entire lobes or lung segments. Thickening of bronchial walls. Mucus plugging: Mucus accumulation in the bronchi can lead to obstruction and recurrent infections. CT may show the presence of bronchial secretions or mucus plugging. Atelectasis: Due to chronic inflammation and obstruction, there may be areas of atelectasis or collapse of lung tissue. Ground-glass opacities: These are areas of hazy increased attenuation on CT, often due to inflammation or edema. Air trapping: Bronchiectasis can lead to impaired airway clearance and air trapping, which may be seen as hyperinflation of the lungs. Bronchial wall irregularity: In severe cases of bronchiectasis, the bronchial walls may appear irregular and nodular, with loss of normal architecture."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bronchial dilatation: The most important finding in bronchiectasis is bronchial dilatation, which may be cylindrical or varicose in shape. The dilatation may be segmental or involve entire lobes or lung segments."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thickening of bronchial walls."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mucus plugging: Mucus accumulation in the bronchi can lead to obstruction and recurrent infections. CT may show the presence of bronchial secretions or mucus plugging."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atelectasis: Due to chronic inflammation and obstruction, there may be areas of atelectasis or collapse of lung tissue."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ground-glass opacities: These are areas of hazy increased attenuation on CT, often due to inflammation or edema."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Air trapping: Bronchiectasis can lead to impaired airway clearance and air trapping, which may be seen as hyperinflation of the lungs."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bronchial wall irregularity: In severe cases of bronchiectasis, the bronchial walls may appear irregular and nodular, with loss of normal architecture."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and C are incorrect."}],"qi":[10298]},{"n":22,"q":"Which of the following statements is accurate regarding the condition of cryptic tuberculosis, in relation to a 65-year-old male who presented with unexplained fever and prolonged respiratory distress despite receiving appropriate treatment?","o":[{"t":"Positive skin tuberculin test and negative chest X-ray"},{"t":"Positive skin tuberculin test and positive chest X-ray"},{"t":"Negative skin tuberculin test and negative chest X-ray"},{"t":"Negative skin tuberculin test and positive chest X-ray"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Negative skin tuberculin test and negative chest X-ray:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cryptic tuberculosis refers to a form of tuberculosis that is difficult to diagnose because it may not show typical findings on chest X-ray or sputum smear examination . Negative skin tuberculin test (absence of a hypersensitivity response to Mycobacterium tuberculosis) and Negative chest X-ray (lacking typical signs of tuberculosis) can be seen in patients with cryptic tuberculosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Negative skin tuberculin test (absence of a hypersensitivity response to Mycobacterium tuberculosis) and Negative chest X-ray (lacking typical signs of tuberculosis) can be seen in patients with cryptic tuberculosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Negative skin tuberculin test (absence of a hypersensitivity response to Mycobacterium tuberculosis) and Negative chest X-ray (lacking typical signs of tuberculosis) can be seen in patients with cryptic tuberculosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":23,"q":"In which medical condition is a ground-glass appearance observed on high-resolution computed tomography (HRCT)?","o":[{"t":"Asbestosis"},{"t":"Silicosis"},{"t":"Anthracosis"},{"t":"Bagassosis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Silicosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The ground-glass appearance on high-resolution computed tomography (HRCT) refers to an imaging pattern in which the lung parenchyma appears hazy, like ground glass , due to partial filling of the air spaces with fluid, inflammatory cells, or fibrosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Silicosis is a progressive lung disease caused by inhalation of crystalline silica dust , commonly seen in occupations such as mining, quarrying , and sandblasting ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In the early stages, HRCT may show ground-glass opacities"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"As the disease progresses, nodular opacities called silicotic nodules develop, typically with an upper lung zone predominance. These nodules may calcify over time, resulting in eggshell calcifications ."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Asbestosis: Asbestosis is a chronic lung disease caused by the inhalation of asbestos fibers. The fibers lead to chronic inflammation and scarring of lung tissue. On HRCT, the characteristic findings in asbestosis include linear opacities that typically have a subpleural and lower lung zone predominance. These opacities may appear as fine reticular lines, thick bands, or honeycombing."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Anthracosis: Anthracosis, or coal workers’ pneumoconiosis, is a lung disease caused by the inhalation and deposition of coal dust particles. The primary HRCT finding in anthracosis is the presence of small, rounded opacities, often referred to as coal macules or coal nodules found in the upper lung zones and can resemble small dots or clusters."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Bagassosis: Bagassosis is an acute hypersensitivity pneumonitis caused by the inhalation of moldy bagasse, HRCT findings in bagassosis can vary but may include ground-glass opacities. Centrilobular nodules, which are small nodules around the central airways, can also be seen. Additionally, air trapping may be observed, indicating small airway involvement."}]},{"n":24,"q":"What can be identified as a contributing factor to the occurrence of exudative pleural effusion?","o":[{"t":"Heart failure"},{"t":"Liver failure"},{"t":"Rheumatoid arthritis"},{"t":"Nephrotic syndrome"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Rheumatoid arthritis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rheumatoid arthritis is an autoimmune disease characterized by chronic inflammation, primarily affecting the joints."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"However, the inflammation associated with rheumatoid arthritis can extend beyond the joints and involve other tissues, including the pleura."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Inflammation of the pleura can cause damage to the blood vessels in the pleural membrane, leading to increased vascular permeability and the leakage of fluid , proteins, and immune cells into the pleural space."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A & B (Heart failure & Liver failure ) are characteristically associated with transudative pleural effusion."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Nephrotic syndrome : Characterized by increased permeability of the glomerular filtration barrier, resulting in the loss of large amounts of protein in the urine . the fluid accumulation typically occurs in the lower extremities ( peripheral edema ) rather than in the pleural cavity. Pleural effusion is not a typical feature of nephrotic syndrome."}]},{"n":25,"q":"Which microorganism is responsible for the symptoms observed in a 70-year-old patient with a history of smoking, presenting with high-grade fever, cough, confusion, and diarrhea? The patient's chest X-ray shows infiltrates in both lower lung fields, while sputum gram stain does not detect any organisms. Additionally, laboratory results show abnormal levels of Na (126mEq/L), AST (62), ALT (56), RBS (112 mg/dl), and serum bilirubin (0.8mg%), as well as a positive HIV test.","o":[{"t":"Streptococcus"},{"t":"Legionella"},{"t":"Pneumocystis jeroveci"},{"t":"Klebsiella"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Legionella:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Legionella cause a severe form of pneumonia known as Legionnaires' disease . Legionella is a strict aerobe, poorly stained with gram stain and infection acquired through inhalation of aerosols."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Immunocompromised individuals are at increaed risk"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The characteristic symptoms include high-grade fever, cough, confusion,diarrhoea and bilateral infiltrates seen on chest X-ray and hyponatremia and elevated liver enzymes ."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Streptococcus: Streptococcus pneumoniae is a common cause of community-acquired pneumonia, but it typically presents with a productive cough with rust-colored sputum . In this case, the sputum gram stain did not detect any organisms, making Streptococcus less likely."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Pneumocystis jiroveci: is a fungal pathogen that causes pneumonia, particularly in immunocompromised individuals, such as those with HIV/AIDS. While the patient in question is HIV positive, the symptoms described and chest X-ray findings of bilateral infiltrates are not specific to Pneumocystis jiroveci pneumonia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Klebsiella: Klebsiella causes pneumonia in individuals with a history of alcoholism or COPD. While the patient has a smoking history, the clinical presentation (including confusion and diarrhea) is inconsistent with Klebsiella pneumoniae infection. Additionally, the absence of organisms on sputum gram stain makes Klebsiella less likely."}]},{"n":26,"q":"What would be the most appropriate course of action for managing a 45-year-old patient, who has been smoking a pack of cigarettes daily for the last 15 years, and is now presenting with hemoptysis? The chest X-ray reveals a 3cm mass in the upper lobe near the lung apex.","o":[{"t":"Bronchoscopy"},{"t":"CT-guided biopsy"},{"t":"Sputum cytology"},{"t":"Sputum for AFB"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - CT-guided biopsy:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CT-guided biopsy is a minimally invasive procedure where a radiologist uses CT imaging to guide the insertion of a needle into the lung mass to obtain a tissue sample for analysis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This method allows for direct sampling of the suspicious area , even if it is located peripherally or near the apex of the lung."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It provides a more accurate diagnosis by providing histopathological information about the nature of the mass, including whether it is cancerous or benign ."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Bronchoscopy: While bronchoscopy can be useful in evaluating central lesions or endobronchial tumors, it may not be effective for evaluating peripheral lung masses, especially those located near the apex.. Therefore, bronchoscopy may not be the best initial step for evaluation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Sputum cytology: It has limited sensitivity for detecting peripheral lung masses , especially smaller ones. In this case, the patient has a 3 cm mass near the apex of the lung, and sputum cytology is unlikely to provide a definitive diagnosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Sputum for AFB: While TB can cause hemoptysis, the appearance of a 3cm upper lobe mass near the apex on chest X-ray is inconsistent with the typical presentation of TB."}]},{"n":27,"q":"Which of the following is not a feature of Gaisböck syndrome?","o":[{"t":"Hypotension"},{"t":"Erythrocytosis"},{"t":"Normal leukocyte counts"},{"t":"Obesity"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Hypotension:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypotension, is not typically associated with Gaisböck syndrome."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Gaisböck syndrome occurs in patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). In these individuals, the primary cause of polycythemia is chronic hypoxemia The body responds to chronic hypoxemia by producing more erythropoietin As a result, the number of red blood cells increases , leading to erythrocytosis. Hypotension is not typically observed. In fact, patients with COPD-related erythrocytosis often have high blood pressure due to theunderlying respiratory disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In these individuals, the primary cause of polycythemia is chronic hypoxemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The body responds to chronic hypoxemia by producing more erythropoietin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"As a result, the number of red blood cells increases , leading to erythrocytosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypotension is not typically observed. In fact, patients with COPD-related erythrocytosis often have high blood pressure due to the underlying respiratory disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In these individuals, the primary cause of polycythemia is chronic hypoxemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The body responds to chronic hypoxemia by producing more erythropoietin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"As a result, the number of red blood cells increases , leading to erythrocytosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypotension is not typically observed. In fact, patients with COPD-related erythrocytosis often have high blood pressure due to the underlying respiratory disease."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Erythrocytosis: Erythrocytosis is a defining feature of Gaisböck syndrome."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Normal leukocyte counts: Normal leukocyte counts are typically seen in Gaisböck syndrome. While the total blood volume increases due to an increase in red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets, the white blood cell count is usually within the normal range."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Obesity: Obesity is a feature of Gaisböck syndrome"}]},{"n":28,"q":"Which of the following is the initial probable diagnosis for a 5-year-old child who has been experiencing recurring respiratory infections with thickened sputum, bronchial wall thickening observed on a chest X-ray, and has had steatorrhea since birth?","o":[{"t":"Hyaline membrane disease"},{"t":"Cystic fibrosis"},{"t":"Alpha 1 anti-trypsin deficiency"},{"t":"Malabsorption syndrome"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Cystic fibrosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cystic fibrosis (CF) is a genetic disorder that primarily affects the lungs and digestive system."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Recurrent respiratory infections, thickened sputum, and bronchial wall thickening as seen on a chest X-ray are all symptoms of cystic fibrosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Steatorrhea since birth is indicative of pancreatic insufficiency , a common manifestation of CF."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Hyaline membrane disease: The presence of a hyaline membrane in the lungs characterises hyaline membrane disease, a respiratory condition that primarily affects premature infants ."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C and D - Alpha 1 anti-trypsin deficiency & Malabsorption syndrome: Child's steatorrhea suggesting malabsorption, the presence of recurrent respiratory infections and bronchial wall thickening makes CF a more likely differential diagnosis."}]},{"n":29,"q":"A 65-year-old chronic smoker came to the medicine outpatient department with complaints of upper chest discomfort and drooping of an eyelid. He also complained of pain radiating to the upper arm and a tingling sensation in the 4th and 5th digits of his left hand. The chest X-ray is given below. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Pancoast tumour"},{"t":"Upper lobe pneumonia"},{"t":"Superior vena cava obstruction"},{"t":"Aspergilloma"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Pancoast tumour:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pancoast tumor is a relatively uncommon primary lung cancer forming in the lung apex and invading the surrounding soft tissues."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This is a tumor that is difficult to treat because of its proximity and frequent metastasis to adjacent structures such as the subclavian vessels, lymphatic system, brachial plexus, spine, second and third ribs, stellate ganglion, and the sympathetic nervous system."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Its proximity to the above-mentioned structures causes the symptoms seen in the patient- pain radiating to the upper arm and a tingling sensation in the 4th and 5th digits of his left hand."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are incorrect."}],"qi":[10304]},{"n":30,"q":"What should be the next appropriate action in the management of an elderly male who has been on prolonged bed rest and is now experiencing symptoms of breathlessness and chest pain?","o":[{"t":"CT pulmonary angiogram"},{"t":"ECG"},{"t":"CT thorax"},{"t":"Echocardiography"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - CT pulmonary angiogram:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"History of bed rest from months in elder individualis and symptoms of brethlessness and chest pain points toward pulmonary embolism"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Elderly with best rest are prone to develop deep vein thrombus which can lead to pulmonary embolism"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Therefore next best investigation in this patient would be CT pulmonary angiography"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CT pulmonary angiogram (CTPA) is used to check for pulmonary embolism or blood clots in the lungs' arteries. A contrast dye is injected into an arm vein, where it travels through the lungs' blood vessels while CT scans are taken."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":31,"q":"A chronic cigarette smoker has now joined a construction company. His pulmonary function test results are given below. What is the most likely diagnosis for this patient? Initial lung volumes were: FEV1-0.9L FVC-1.9L FEV1/FVC-0.4 After bronchodilation: FEV1-1.9L FVC-3.9L FEV1/FVC-0.4","o":[{"t":"Vascular disease with bronchodilator reversibility"},{"t":"Restrictive lung disease with bronchodilator reversibility"},{"t":"Restrictive lung disease without bronchodilator reversibility"},{"t":"Obstructive disease with bronchodilator reversibility"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Obstructive disease with bronchodilator reversibility:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In people with asthma or chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), bronchodilator reversibility test is commonly used. The purpose of the test is to determine whether the subject's airflow has improved as a result of taking the bronchodilator medication. The given pulmonary function test results indicate a diagnosis of obstructive lung disease with bronchodilator reversibility as the FEV1/FVC is less than 0.7 so it is an obstructive disease and after bronchodilation there is an increase of >12% in FEV1 hence it is reversible. Improved lung volumes after bronchodilation indicate a response to the bronchodilator which is a feature of COPD."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Vascular disease with bronchodilator reversibility: Vascular disease does not typically present with bronchodilator reversibility, so this option is unlikely."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Restrictive lung disease with bronchodilator reversibility: Restrictive lung diseases are characterized by reduced lung volumes, but the given lung volumes do not suggest restriction. Additionally, improved lung volumes after bronchodilation indicate a response to the bronchodilator, which is not typically seen in restrictive lung diseases."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Restrictive lung disease without bronchodilator reversibility: The lung volumes provided do not indicate restriction. Additionally, the lack of improvement in lung volumes after bronchodilation contradicts this option."}]},{"n":32,"q":"Which location in the abdomen is most frequently affected by tuberculosis?","o":[{"t":"Rectum"},{"t":"Colon"},{"t":"Small intestine"},{"t":"Ileocecal junction"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Ileocecal junction:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The ileocecal junction refers to the area where the small intestine (ileum) meets the large intestine (cecum)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is a common site for the development of tuberculosis infection in the abdomen."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tuberculosis is an infectious disease caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis, and it can affect various organs and systems in the body, including the gastrointestinal tract."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A, B and C - Rectum, Colon & Small intestine:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Although tuberculosis can affect the rectum , colon and the small intestine , the ileocaecal junction is the most common site."}]},{"n":33,"q":"What does the given ECG show?","o":[{"t":"P-pulmonale"},{"t":"Ventricular bigeminy"},{"t":"Electrical alternans"},{"t":"Improper calibration"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - P-pulmonale:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In the given ECG, P-wave amplitude ‡2.5 mm in leads II, III, and aVF."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"P-wave axis deviation towards the right side indicates P-pulmonale."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are incorrect."}],"qi":[10311]},{"n":34,"q":"A 65-year-old female patient weighing 60 kg is on mechanical ventilation for ARDS secondary to urosepsis. The respiratory parameters are as follows: tidal volume – 360 mL; frequency – 30 breaths/min; PEEP - 5 cm of H2O; and FiO2-90%. The arterial blood gas findings are as follows; paO2-50 mmHg; paCO2-38 mmHg; and pH-7.38. What is the next step?","o":[{"t":"Reduce FiO2"},{"t":"Increase tidal volume"},{"t":"Increase respiratory rate"},{"t":"Increase PEEP"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Increase PEEP:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In the given scenario, the patient is experiencing ARDS (Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome) secondary to urosepsis and is on mechanical ventilation. The arterial blood gas (ABG) results show a low paO 2 level of 50 mmHg, indicating hypoxemia.To improveoxygenation in ARDS, the next step would be to increase the positive end-expiratorypressure (PEEP). PEEP helps to recruit collapsed alveoli, improve lung compliance, andmaintain alveolar patency. By increasing PEEP, it helps to open up more lung units, improveoxygenation, and increase functional residual capacity (FRC)."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Reduce FiO2: The FiO2 is already set at 90% to provide a high concentration of oxygen to the patient. Further reducing FiO2 may compromise oxygenation and worsen hypoxemia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Increase tidal volume: Increasing tidal volume can lead to increased risk of ventilator-induced lung injury (VILI) and barotrauma. It is generally recommended to use lower tidal volumes (around 6 mL/kg of ideal body weight) in ARDS to minimize complications."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Increase respiratory rate: Increasing the respiratory rate may improve minute ventilation but does not directly address the issue of hypoxemia. It is not the ideal intervention to improve oxygenation in ARDS."}]},{"n":35,"q":"An increased anion gap is seen in:","o":[{"t":"Respiratory acidosis"},{"t":"Respiratory alkalosis"},{"t":"Metabolic acidosis"},{"t":"Metabolic alkalosis"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Metabolic acidosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Metabolic acidosis occurs when there is an excess of acid or a loss of bicarbonate (HCO 3 -) in the body, leading to a decrease in the bicarbonate concentration and a decrease in pH."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This decrease is compensated by an increase in chloride ions, resulting in an increased anion gap."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and D are not associated with an increased anion gap."}]},{"n":36,"q":"Hamman sign is seen in:","o":[{"t":"Pneumoperitoneum"},{"t":"Pneumopericardium"},{"t":"Pneumomediastinum"},{"t":"Hydropneumothorax"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Pneumomediastinum:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hamman's sign is heard during auscultation of the chest and is associated with a specific condition called pneumomediastinum."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It refers to the crunching or crackling sound synchronous with the heartbeat, known as crepitus, caused by air within the mediastinum."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The sound is best heard along the left sternal border."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Option A, B and D are not associated with the Hamman's sign."}]},{"n":37,"q":"The given image shows a normal graph on the left and the patient’s graph on the right. Which of the following diagnosis can be inferred from the graph?","o":[{"t":"Chest wall neuromuscular disease"},{"t":"Sarcoidosis"},{"t":"Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis"},{"t":"Bronchiectasis"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Bronchiectasis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bronchiectasis is characterized by permanent dilation and t hickening of the bronchial walls."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Obstructive ventilatory impairment may be observed, leading to a reduced FEV1 and an decreased FVC, resulting in a decreased FEV1/FVC ratio."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The volume/time graph may show a reduced peak expiratory flow rate and prolonged expiration."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Chest wall neuromuscular disease: Decreased respiratory muscle strength and endurance can lead to reduced FEV1 and FVC values but FEV1/FVC ratio is normal or increased."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Sarcoidosis: Pulmonary involvement in sarcoidosis can lead to restrictive lung disease. Both FEV1 and FVC may be reduced, but the FEV1/FVC ratio remains normal or even increased."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis: Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis is characterized by progressive scarring of lung tissue. It typically presents as a restrictive lung disease. Both FEV1 and FVC may be reduced proportionally, maintaining a normal or near-normal FEV1/FVC ratio. The volume/time graph may show reduced lung volumes and a flattened slope during inspiration and expiration."}],"qi":[10317]},{"n":38,"q":"A 40-year-old man presents with daytime sleepiness and impaired concentration and memory. On examination his BMI is 41 kg/m2, BP is 160/100 mm Hg. His awake ABG analysis is given: PaO2= 66mm Hg, PaCO2= 50 mm Hg, HCO3= 28 mEq/L. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Obstructive sleep apnea"},{"t":"Narcolepsy"},{"t":"Obesity hypo-ventilation syndrome"},{"t":"Central sleep apnea"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Obesity hypoventilation syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"OHS is defined by raised 'awake' arterial pressure of carbon dioxide levels in patients with obesity in whom alternative causes of hypercapnia and hypoventilation have been excluded."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This is caused due to obesity causing a restriction in lung filling. This causes hypoventilation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this patient with hypercapnia while awake, with compensatory metabolic alkalosis, the diagnosis is likely OHS."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Obstructive sleep apnea:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) is a disorder that is characterized by obstructive apneas and hypopneas due to repetitive collapse of the upper airway during sleep."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"While most patients with OHS will have Obstructive sleep apnea(90%) the clinical features here point to a diagnosis of OHS."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The clinical features against OSA include hypercapnia and hyoxemia in an awake individual ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"OSA patients will demonstrate hyoxemia only while asleep and not while awake."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Therefore even though this patient is likely to have OSA, OHS is the better answer given the scenario and clinical information provided."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Narcolepsy:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Narcolepsy is characterized by excessive daytime sleepiness, sudden loss of muscle tone (cataplexy), and disrupted sleep patterns. While impaired concentration and memory can be seen in narcolepsy. ABG is normal."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Central sleep apnea:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Central sleep apnea is a condition in which there is a lack of respiratory effort during sleep, leading to pauses in breathing. It is different from obstructive sleep apnea, where the airway is physically blocked. Impaired concentration and memory can occur in central sleep apnea. The most likely diagnosis in this case is Obesity hypoventilation syndrome (OHS) due to the combination of obesity (BMI 41 kg/m2), daytime sleepiness, impaired concentration, and the ABG results showing elevated PaCO 2 (50 mm Hg)."}]},{"n":39,"q":"Which membrane channel is mainly affected in Cystic fibrosis?","o":[{"t":"Sodium"},{"t":"Chloride"},{"t":"Calcium"},{"t":"Potassium"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Chloride (Cl):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The defect in cystic fibrosis is specifically associated with the CFTR chloride channel. Mutations in the CFTR gene result in a defective or absent CFTR protein, impairing the movement of chloride ions across cell membranes. This disruption of chloride transport leads to the characteristic features of CF, including the production of thick and sticky mucus in various organs."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":40,"q":"A 68-year-old man presents with cough with yellowish sputum. Auscultation revealed bronchial breath sounds. He is hemodynamically stable and not confused. On examination, his respiratory rate is 20/min and blood pressure is 110/70 mmHg. Lab reports show urea levels of 44 mg/dL. What is the next best step in the management of this patient?","o":[{"t":"Given antibiotics and send the patient home"},{"t":"Admit to ICU without mechanical ventilation"},{"t":"Admit to ICU with invasive mechanical ventilation"},{"t":"Consider admission in a non ICU setting"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Consider admission in a non ICU setting:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"CURB-65 score:"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Confusion 1 point"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Urea > 7 mmol/L (19 mg/dL) 1 point Respiratory rate ‡ 30 breaths/min 1 point"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Blood pressure (systolic < 90 mmHg or diastolic £ 60 1 point mmHg) Age ‡ 65 years 1 point"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Interpretation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Score Treatment"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"0 OPD basis"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"1-2 IPD basis ‡3 ICU basis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The CURB-65 score for the patient in the above case is 2 (urea > 19 mg/dL and age ‡ 65 years)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Considering admission in a non ICU setting should be the treatment plan for the patient."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options: Options A, B, and C are incorrect. Refer to the explanation of the correct answer."}]},{"n":41,"q":"What might be the potential reason for the occurrence of carpo-pedal spasm in a woman experiencing hyperventilation due to hysteria?","o":[{"t":"Respiratory acidosis"},{"t":"Metabolic acidosis"},{"t":"Respiratory alkalosis"},{"t":"Metabolic alkalosis"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Respiratory alkalosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Respiratory alkalosis occurs when there is a decrease in carbon dioxide (CO2) levels in the blood, leading to an increase in alkalinity. It can be caused by hyperventilation, anxiety, or high-altitude exposure. Hyperventilation causes excessive elimination of carbon dioxide, leading to a decrease in blood CO2 levels. This results in respiratory alkalosis. Symptoms may include lightheadedness, dizziness, tingling sensations, and carpo-pedal spasms. In this case, the patient's hyperventilation and subsequent carpo-pedal spasms suggest respiratory alkalosis as the likely cause."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In a hyperventilating patient, Options A, B and D are unlikely."}]},{"n":42,"q":"What is the probable diagnosis for a patient who has been experiencing fever, coughing at night, difficulty breathing, and wheezing for a duration of 4 weeks, along with an absolute eosinophil count exceeding 5000/microliter and a miliary pattern observed on the chest x-ray?","o":[{"t":"Bronchial asthma"},{"t":"Miliary tuberculosis"},{"t":"Tropical pulmonary eosinophilia"},{"t":"Hypersensitivity pneumonitis"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Tropical pulmonary eosinophilia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tropical pulmonary eosinophilia (TPE) is a hypersensitivity reaction to filarial parasites (Wuchereria bancrofti or Brugia malayi) and their products. It is more commonly seen in individuals living in endemic areas. Symptoms include fever, nocturnal cough, breathlessness, wheezing, and an increased eosinophil count (>500/µL). The miliary pattern on chest X-ray is typical of TPE."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Therefore, the diagnosis is TPE."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Bronchial asthma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bronchial asthma is a chronic respiratory condition characterized by inflammation and narrowing of the airways, leading to symptoms such as wheezing, coughing (often nocturnal), breathlessness, and occasionally fever."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"However, the presence of a miliary pattern on chest X-ray is not typical of bronchial asthma. Therefore, bronchial asthma is less likely to be the diagnosis in this case."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Miliary tuberculosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Miliary tuberculosis is a severe form of tuberculosis (TB) in which the infection spreads throughout the body, including the lungs. It is characterized by the dissemination of tiny, millimeter-sized lesions throughout various organs, including the lungs, giving a miliary pattern on chest X-ray."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms can include fever, cough, breathlessness, and weight loss. The elevated absolute eosinophil count is not typical of miliary tuberculosis. Therefore, miliary tuberculosis is less likely to be the diagnosis in this case."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Hypersensitivity pneumonitis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypersensitivity pneumonitis (HP), also known as extrinsic allergic alveolitis, is an immune-mediated lung disease caused by the inhalation of various organic substances such as dust, molds, or animal proteins. Symptoms typically include fever, cough (often nocturnal), breathlessness, and wheezing. The absolute eosinophil count can be elevated in some cases. However, the presence of a miliary pattern on chest X-ray is not typical of HP. Therefore, HP is less likely to be the diagnosis in this case."}]},{"n":43,"q":"A 10-year-old child with AIDS presented with fever and a productive cough. On auscultation, bronchial breath sounds and crepitations were heard in the right infra-scapular region. Chest x-ray showed right lower lobe consolidation. The CD4 count was 55 cells/mm3. What is the most likely causative organism for this condition?","o":[{"t":"Pneumocystis jirovecii"},{"t":"Streptococcus pneumonia"},{"t":"Staphylococcus aureus"},{"t":"Mycoplasma"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Streptococcus pneumonia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Streptococcus pneumoniae is a bacterium and a common cause of bacterial pneumonia in both immunocompetent and immunocompromised individuals. In the context of a child with AIDS, a low CD4 count, fever, productive cough, and consolidation on chest X-ray, Streptococcus pneumoniae is the most common and likely causative organism. It can cause lobar pneumonia, as indicated by the right lower lobe consolidation on the chest X-ray."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Pneumocystis jirovecii: Pneumocystis jirovecii is a fungus that commonly causes pneumonia in immunocompromised individuals, especially those with AIDS. It typically presents with non-productive cough, shortness of breath, and bilateral diffuse infiltrates on chest X-ray. While Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PJP) is a common opportunistic infection in patients with AIDS, the clinical presentation described in the scenario, including fever, productive cough, and unilateral consolidation, is not typical of PJP. Therefore, Pneumocystis jirovecii is not the most likely causative organism in this case."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Staphylococcus aureus: Staphylococcus aureus is another bacterium that can cause pneumonia in immunocompromised individuals, including those with AIDS. It is known for causing severe infections, including necrotizing pneumonia. While Staphylococcus aureus can be a potential pathogen, S.pneumoniae is more commonly associated with this presentation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Mycoplasma: Mycoplasma pneumoniae is an atypical bacterium that can cause pneumonia, particularly in children and young adults. It often presents with a milder respiratory illness, including a persistent cough, headache, and sore throat. The chest X-ray findings typically show patchy infiltrates rather than lobar consolidation. Although Mycoplasma can cause pneumonia, it is not the most likely causative organism in this case, given the clinical presentation and imaging findings."}]},{"n":44,"q":"N95 respirator is used for:","o":[{"t":"Droplet precautions"},{"t":"Airborne precautions"},{"t":"Contact precautions"},{"t":"All of the above"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Airborne precautions:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Airborne infections are one of the five modes of transmission of diseases. N95 masks protect from most diseases that arise through airborne transmission."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Droplet precautions: N95 masks do not provide complete protection from droplet spread infection. The expelled droplets can cause infection on coming in contact with even the skin or conjunctiva."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Contact precautions: Using an N95 respirator alone is not essential to preventing diseases that could be transmitted through contact with a patient."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - All of the above: N95 alone does not protect from diseases requiring contact and droplet precautions. So this option is incorrect."}]},{"n":45,"q":"Which of the following statements accurately describes the initial management of a 60-year-old male patient, previously diagnosed with COPD, who is currently experiencing an acute exacerbation and has been admitted to the ICU?","o":[{"t":"Non-invasive PPV should be given"},{"t":"Invasive PPV should be given"},{"t":"Oxygen is not indicated"},{"t":"Permissive hypoapnia is allowed"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Non-invasive PPV should be given:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In the initial management of a patient with an acute exacerbation of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), non-invasive positive pressure ventilation (NIPPV) is a recommended intervention. NIPPV involves the application of positive pressure through a mask or nasal prongs to assist with breathing, improve oxygenation, and alleviate respiratory distress."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Invasive PPV should be given: Invasive positive pressure ventilation (IPPV) involves intubating the patient and providing mechanical ventilation through an endotracheal tube. In the initial management of an acute exacerbation of COPD, NIPPV is preferred over IPPV, as it can often effectively improve oxygenation and ventilation without the need for invasive procedures."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Oxygen is not indicated: Oxygen therapy is a mainstay in the management of patients with COPD exacerbation"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Permissive hypercapnia and not 'hypocapnia' is allowed: Permissive hypercapnia refers to a ventilation strategy where a higher than normal level of carbon dioxide (hypercapnia) is tolerated to avoid potential lung injury caused by high ventilatory pressures."}]},{"n":46,"q":"In a patient with suspected metabolic acidosis, which of the following arterial blood gas (ABG) findings is most likely to be observed?","o":[{"t":"Increased pH, decreased bicarbonate (HCO 3 -)"},{"t":"Decreased pH, increased bicarbonate (HCO 3 -)"},{"t":"Decreased pH, decreased bicarbonate (HCO 3 -)"},{"t":"Increased pH, increased bicarbonate (HCO 3 -)"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Decreased pH, decreased bicarbonate (HCO 3 -)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Metabolic acidosis is a condition characterized by a decrease in blood pH and a decrease in bicarbonate (HCO 3 -) levels."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Metabolic Acidosis: This type of acid-base imbalance occurs when there is an excess of acid in the body or a loss of bicarbonate. Causes include conditions such as diabetic ketoacidosis, lactic acidosis, renal failure, and ingestion of certain toxins."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Therefore, in a patient with metabolic acidosis, you would expect to see an ABG with a decreased pH and a decreased bicarbonate level. This combination indicates the presence of a metabolic acidosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Option A, B and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":47,"q":"What should be the next course of action for a 20-year-old male who arrives at the emergency department with difficulty breathing and low blood pressure after experiencing an injury? The patient exhibits subcutaneous emphysema and there is no air movement observed in the right lung.","o":[{"t":"Start IV fluids with large bore cannula"},{"t":"Needle decompression into the 5 th intercostal space anterior to the midaxillary line"},{"t":"Take the patient to the ICU and intubate him"},{"t":"Start positive pressure ventilation"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Needle decompression into the 5 th intercostal space anterior to the midaxillary line:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Needle decompression into the 5th intercostal space anterior to the midaxillary line: This is the correct next step in managing the patient. The clinical presentation, including respiratory distress, hypotension, subcutaneous emphysema, and absent air entry on the affected side, is highly suggestive of a tension pneumothorax. Needle decompression is an emergency procedure performed to release the trapped air in the pleural space, providing immediate relief and improving the patient's respiratory and hemodynamic status. It involves inserting a large-bore needle (such as an 18-gauge) into the 5th intercostal space in the anterior axillary line to decompress the pleural cavity."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":48,"q":"A 55-year-old male presented with tachypnea and mental confusion. His blood sugar is 350mg/dl, pH-7.2, HCO3-10, pCO2-30. What is the metabolic abnormality?","o":[{"t":"Metabolic alkalosis"},{"t":"Metabolic acidosis"},{"t":"Respiratory alkalosis"},{"t":"Respiratory acidosis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Metabolic acidosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Metabolic acidosis is characterized by a decrease in pH and bicarbonate (HCO3-) levels in the blood. In this case, the low pH of 7.2 and low HCO3- level of 10 indicate an acidotic state."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":49,"q":"Which among the following is not a part of Well’s score?","o":[{"t":"Heart Rate"},{"t":"Respiratory rate"},{"t":"Hemoptysis"},{"t":"Clinical sign of DVT"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Respiratory rate:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is not a component of the Wells score."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Heart Rate: It is a component of the Wells score"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Hemoptysis: It is a component of the Wells score"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Clinical sign of DVT: It is a component of the Wells score"}]},{"n":50,"q":"Which of the following is shown in the image below?","o":[{"t":"Pleural effusion"},{"t":"Pneumothorax"},{"t":"Consolidation"},{"t":"Cavity"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Pneumothorax:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The given image is an X-ray of the lungs/thorax of a patient with left sided pneumothorax. In Pneumothorax, air collects in the pleural cavity. Air can enter the pleural cavity when a small opening occurs on the chest wall. An X-Ray of Pneumothorax can be identified by: Deep sulcus sign White pleural line Absent lung markings"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Deep sulcus sign"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"White pleural line"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Absent lung markings"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Deep sulcus sign"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"White pleural line"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Absent lung markings"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect"}],"qi":[10328]},{"n":51,"q":"A 60 year old man with a history of COPD presents with sudden onset increased shortness of breath. His ABG analysis reveals pH – 7.24; pCO2 – 60 mm Hg, pO2 – 52 mmHg, HCO3 – 27 mmol/L. What is the most appropriate treatment form the following:","o":[{"t":"Endotracheal intubation"},{"t":"Invasive positive pressure ventilation"},{"t":"Non-invasive positive pressure ventilation (NIPPV)"},{"t":"Lung volume reduction surgery"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Non-invasive positive pressure ventilation (NIPPV):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Non-invasive positive pressure ventilation is the right choice."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Here, the arterial blood gas analysis shows that the patient was in respiratory acidosis, which is due to an increase in carbon dioxide. this condition can be managed by administering Non-Invasive Positive Pressure Ventilation through BiPaP and CPaP"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and D are incorrect and not indicated in this patient at present. NIPPV would be the most appropriate choice of treatment at this juncture."}]},{"n":52,"q":"A 72-year-old man presented with weight loss, decreased appetite, and iron deficiency anemia. Chest x-ray was done and given below. What is the diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Lung metastasis"},{"t":"Bronchogenic carcinoma"},{"t":"Miliary tuberculosis"},{"t":"Mesothelioma"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Lung metastasis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lung metastasis is the correct answer. The radiological imaging reveals Canon Ball Metastasis of the lung likely secondary to malignancies such as Renal Cell Carcinoma . The image shows large, multiple, well-circumscribed, round pulmonary mets that look like cannon balls, hence the name."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are incorrect."}],"qi":[10335]},{"n":53,"q":"A 55 year old shipbuilding worker presents with dyspnea.He has been working in this industry for the past 25 years. Which is the most specific malignancy that we can expect in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Bronchogenic carcinoma"},{"t":"Malignant mesothelioma"},{"t":"Small cell carcinoma of lung"},{"t":"Squamous cell carcinoma of lung"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Malignant mesothelioma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is a tumor of mesothelial cells. It is the most specific malignancy seen in asbestosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Bronchogenic carcinoma: While bronchogenic carcinoma is the most common lung malignancy, it is not the most specific malignancy associated with asbestos exposure (shipbuilding fi asbestos exposure)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":54,"q":"A 60-year-old male patient comes to your clinic with an 8-month history of shortness of breath and non-productive cough. He has smoked half a pack of cigarettes for the past 30 years. On physical examination, clubbing is seen and fine crackles are heard on both sides during inspiration. You perform pulmonary function tests and it shows an FEV1/FVC ratio of 92%. The CT scan of the chest is shown below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Bronchial asthma"},{"t":"Pulmonary fibrosis"},{"t":"Pulmonary tuberculosis"},{"t":"Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Pulmonary fibrosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In the above case, the patient presents with shortness of breath and has a history of chronic smoking, clubbing, and bilateral crackling sounds on auscultation. The most probable diagnosis is that of pulmonary fibrosis. The image also shows honeycombing and traction bronchiectasis which are characteristic CT findings seen in patients with pulmonary fibrosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect and do not present the features described above."}],"qi":[10342]},{"n":55,"q":"In patients affected by COVID-19, which pattern is frequently observed in chest CT scans?","o":[{"t":"Interlobular septal thickening"},{"t":"Air bronchograms"},{"t":"Ground glass opacity"},{"t":"Crazy paving pattern"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Ground glass opacity:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ground Glass Opacities towards the periphery are the earliest investigative manifestation in COVID 19."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Crazy pavement pattern and reverse halo sign is noticed."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"GGOs are hazy areas with slightly increased density without obscuration of underlying bronchial and vascular margins."},{"k":"img","v":10449},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":56,"q":"A patient was admitted to the ICU 48 hours after an accident in which he sustained a fracture of the femur. The oxygen saturation in the rebreathing unit was 100% but his SpO2 remained at 60%. The patient is in a state of confusion. Diffuse petechiae are noted on examination. Chest radiograph showed lung fields to be clear. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Pulmonary embolism"},{"t":"Fat embolism"},{"t":"ARDS"},{"t":"Occult pneumothorax"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Fat embolism:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In the above case, there is the presence of respiratory insufficiency, altered mental status, and diffuse petechial rash after fracture of the femur. This indicates that the most probable diagnosis is that of fat embolism."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect and the presentation is more characteristic of fat embolism."}]},{"n":57,"q":"Which of the following imaging tests should be performed to confirm a diagnosis of pulmonary embolism in a patient?","o":[{"t":"Doppler echocardiography"},{"t":"CT pulmonary angiography"},{"t":"Ventilation perfusion scan of lungs"},{"t":"MR pulmonary angiography"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - CT pulmonary angiography:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The most important imaging test for pulmonary embolism is CT pulmonary angiography. It can be used to confirm a diagnosis of PE in suspected patients."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":58,"q":"In the management of a 35-year-old female patient with class II pulmonary hypertension who has obtained a negative vasoreactive test, which of the following options is employed in the subsequent step?","o":[{"t":"Iloprost"},{"t":"Ambrisentan"},{"t":"Nifedipine"},{"t":"Epoprostenol"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Ambrisentan:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In a patient with class II pulmonary hypertension who has a negative vasoreactive test, the next step of management would typically involve the use of specific pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH) medications."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Among the options provided, the most appropriate choice for the next step of management in this scenario would be option b. Ambrisentan is a selective endothelin receptor antagonist that is commonly used in the treatment of pulmonary arterial hypertension. It helps to improve exercise capacity and delay disease progression in PAH patients. It acts by blocking the effects of endothelin, a substance that promotes vasoconstriction and cell proliferation in the pulmonary arteries."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Iloprost: Iloprost is a prostacyclin analogue that can be administered intravenously or inhaled. It is often used in more advanced stages of PAH or in patients who do not respond adequately to other therapies."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Nifedipine: Nifedipine is a calcium channel blocker that can help to relax and dilate blood vessels. While calcium channel blockers can be effective in some cases of PAH, they are generally reserved for patients who demonstrate a positive response during a vasoreactive test."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Epoprostenol: Epoprostenol is a prostacyclin that is administered continuously via intravenous infusion. It is typically used in severe cases of PAH or in patients who do not respond to other therapies."}]},{"n":59,"q":"What is the recommended course of action for a newly diagnosed HIV patient who is experiencing symptoms of fever, cough, and haemoptysis, and whose sputum sample has revealed the presence of acid-fast bacilli?","o":[{"t":"Start ART if CD4<200"},{"t":"Start ART and then ATT after 2 weeks"},{"t":"Start ATT and then ART after 2 weeks"},{"t":"Start ATT and ART simultaneously"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Start ATT and then ART after 2 weeks:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this scenario, the presence of acid-fast bacilli in the sputum sample suggests a potential co-infection with tuberculosis (TB). It is common for individuals with HIV to develop TB due to their weakened immune systems."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The recommended approach in managing HIV-TB co-infection is to initiate ATT first and then after 2 weeks ART. This approach is based on evidence showing improved outcomes and reduced mortality rates."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":60,"q":"What could be the potential cause when a patient presents with hypoxemia but a normal alveolar-arterial oxygen gradient?","o":[{"t":"Right to left shunt"},{"t":"Ventilation/perfusion mismatch"},{"t":"Hypoventilation"},{"t":"Alveolar membrane damage"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Hypoventilation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In a patient with hypoxemia and a normal alveolar-arterial oxygen gradient, hypoventilation is the likely cause. Hypoventilation refers to inadequate ventilation of the alveoli, leading to a reduced exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide. This can result from various factors such as respiratory muscle weakness, central nervous system depression, or mechanical abnormalities of the chest wall."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Right-to-left shunt : A right-to-left shunt refers to the mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood, bypassing the normal oxygenation process in the lungs. It results in hypoxemia, but it would typically be associated with an increased alveolar-arterial oxygen gradient rather than a normal gradient."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Ventilation/perfusion (V/Q) mismatch: V/Q mismatch occurs when there is a mismatch between the ventilation of the alveoli and the perfusion of blood to the capillaries surrounding the alveoli. It can lead to hypoxemia, and depending on the severity, it may cause an increased alveolar-arterial oxygen gradient."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Alveolar membrane damage: Damage to the alveolar membrane, such as in conditions like pulmonary fibrosis or acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS), can impair the diffusion of oxygen from the alveoli into the bloodstream. This would typically result in an increased alveolar-arterial oxygen gradient rather than a normal gradient."}]},{"n":61,"q":"What is the likely diagnosis based on the presented flow-volume loop.","o":[{"t":"Variable small airway obstruction"},{"t":"Fixed central airway obstruction"},{"t":"Variable intrathoracic obstruction"},{"t":"Variable extrathoracic obstruction"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Fixed central airway obstruction:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fixed central airway obstruction refers to a condition where there is a persistent and constant narrowing or blockage of the central airways within the lungs. This obstruction occurs at a fixed or unchanging location and does not vary with breathing or changes in lung volume. Various factors, including tumors, strictures, foreign bodies, or scarring within the central airways can cause it. It leads to impaired airflow and can result in symptoms such as shortness of breath, wheezing, and coughing."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect."}],"qi":[10350]},{"n":62,"q":"Acid-base imbalance is suspected in a patient. Which of the following would you use to determine it? 1. Arterial pH 2. Venous pH 3. Venous pO2 4. Venous pCO2","o":[{"t":"1,2"},{"t":"1,2,3"},{"t":"1,2,4"},{"t":"1,2,3,4"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - 1, 2:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"To determine acid-base imbalances in a patient, the following parameters can be used: Arterial pH: Arterial blood gas (ABG) analysis measures arterial pH, which reflects the acidity or alkalinity of the blood. It is a direct indicator of acid-base status. Venous pH: Venous blood gas (VBG) analysis can also provide information about pH levels in venous blood. While arterial pH is the gold standard, venous pH can be used as an alternative in certain clinical situations when arterial blood sampling is challenging or not readily available."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Arterial pH: Arterial blood gas (ABG) analysis measures arterial pH, which reflects the acidity or alkalinity of the blood. It is a direct indicator of acid-base status."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Venous pH: Venous blood gas (VBG) analysis can also provide information about pH levels in venous blood. While arterial pH is the gold standard, venous pH can be used as an alternative in certain clinical situations when arterial blood sampling is challenging or not readily available."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These two parameters can help determine a patient's acid-base imbalance."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Arterial pH: Arterial blood gas (ABG) analysis measures arterial pH, which reflects the acidity or alkalinity of the blood. It is a direct indicator of acid-base status."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Venous pH: Venous blood gas (VBG) analysis can also provide information about pH levels in venous blood. While arterial pH is the gold standard, venous pH can be used as an alternative in certain clinical situations when arterial blood sampling is challenging or not readily available."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":63,"q":"Which of the following findings from a pleural fluid aspirate will suggest an exudative pleural effusion? Normal values Total S. Protein: 6-8 gm% S.LDH: 140-280 IU S. glucose: 140-200","o":[{"t":"Pleural: Serum protein ratio > 0.5, pleural: Serum LDH ratio > 0.6 or pleural LDH > 2/3"},{"t":"Pleural fluid protein = 4.5 gm% and glucose = 30 mg%"},{"t":"Pleural fluid LDH = 90IU and glucose = 30 mg%"},{"t":"Pleural fluid protein = 3.5gm% and glucose = 90 mg%"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Pleural: Serum protein ratio > 0.5, pleural: Serum LDH ratio > 0.6 or pleural LDH > 2/3:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"An effusion with any of the following characteristics is classified as an exudate; pleural: Serum protein ratio > 0.5, pleural: Serum LDH ratio > 0.6 or pleural LDH > 2/3 of the upper limit of normal for the serum. This is known as Light's criteria."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":64,"q":"A 45-year-old female presents to the emergency department with the following arterial blood gas (ABG) results: pH 7.55, PaCO2 40 mmHg, HCO3- 32 mEq/L. Which of the following conditions is most likely responsible for these findings?","o":[{"t":"Metabolic acidosis"},{"t":"Metabolic alkalosis"},{"t":"Respiratory acidosis"},{"t":"Respiratory alkalosis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Metabolic alkalosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Based on the given ABG results, the most likely condition responsible for the findings is metabolic alkalosis (Option B)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The pH is elevated (>7.45) and the bicarbonate (HCO 3 -) level is increased (32 mEq/L), indicating a primary metabolic alkalosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":65,"q":"The flow - volume curve of a patient after spirometry is given below. The most likely diagnosis in this patient is:","o":[{"t":"Asbestosis"},{"t":"COPD"},{"t":"Myasthenia gravis"},{"t":"Vocal cord paralysis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - COPD (Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The vignette depicts the characteristic flow volume loop seen in patients with COPD. Patients with COPD have the following lung volume changes which are responsible for this pattern: Increased residual volume Increased total lung capacity Decreased PEFR"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased residual volume"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased total lung capacity"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Decreased PEFR"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased residual volume"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased total lung capacity"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Decreased PEFR"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect."}],"qi":[10357]},{"n":66,"q":"Addison's disease is characterized by all of the following symptoms except?","o":[{"t":"Hyperglycemia"},{"t":"Hyponatremia"},{"t":"Hyperkalemia"},{"t":"Hypotension"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Hyperglycemia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hyperglycemia, or high blood sugar levels, is not typically associated with Addison's disease. In fact, individuals with Addison's disease may experience hypoglycemia (low blood sugar) due to the inadequate production of cortisol, a hormone involved in regulating blood sugar levels."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Hyponatremia: Hyponatremia refers to low levels of sodium in the blood. It is commonly observed in Addison's disease because insufficient levels of aldosterone, a hormone produced by the adrenal glands, can lead to sodium loss. Thus, hyponatremia is associated withAddison's disease."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Hyperkalemia: Hyperkalemia refers to high levels of potassium in the blood. It is a characteristic feature of Addison's disease. Inadequate levels of aldosterone can impair the excretion of potassium by the kidneys, leading to its accumulation in the bloodstream."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Hypotension: Hypotension, or low blood pressure, is commonly observed in Addison's disease. The insufficient production of adrenal hormones, including cortisol and aldosterone, can result in fluid and electrolyte imbalances, leading to decreased blood volume and hypotension."}]},{"n":67,"q":"Which of the following pneumoconiosis is associated with an increased incidence of tuberculosis?","o":[{"t":"Asbestosis"},{"t":"Silicosis"},{"t":"Coal worker’s pneumoniosis"},{"t":"Berryliosis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Silicosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Among the given options, the pneumoconiosis associated with an increased incidence of tuberculosis is \"Silicosis.\""},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Silicosis is a lung disease caused by the inhalation of crystalline silica particles. It commonly affects individuals working in occupations such as mining, sandblasting, and stone cutting. Silicosis can impair the immune system and increase the risk of developing tuberculosis. The presence of silica particles in the lungs can create an environment conducive to Mycobacterium tuberculosis infection and growth."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":68,"q":"A patient with a history of fever for the past 4 days and new onset of breathlessness was admitted to ICU as he was deteriorating. He was unresponsive to the incremental flow rate of oxygen and his oxygen saturation was progressively decreasing. A chest x-ray was ordered and the findings are shown in the image below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Atelectasis"},{"t":"Mediastinitis"},{"t":"Pulmonary fibrosis"},{"t":"ARDS"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - ARDS:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Based on the provided information and the chest X-ray findings, the most likely diagnosis for the patient is Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ARDS is a severe lung condition characterized by widespread inflammation and damage to the alveoli (air sacs) in the lungs. It can be triggered by various factors, including infection, sepsis, trauma, or inhalation injury. The key features of ARDS include acute onset of severe hypoxemia (low oxygen levels) that is refractory to supplemental oxygen, bilateral pulmonary infiltrates on imaging (such as the chest X-ray), and impaired lung compliance."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Atelectasis: Atelectasis refers to the collapse or incomplete expansion of lung tissue. It can result from blockage of the airways, compression of the lung, or surfactant deficiency. While atelectasis can cause localized or segmental lung collapse, it typically does not lead to widespread bilateral infiltrates and severe hypoxemia as seen in the patient's presentation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Mediastinitis: Mediastinitis refers to inflammation or infection of the mediastinum, which is the area between the lungs. It is usually caused by an infection that spreads from the nearby structures or as a complication of a surgical procedure. While mediastinitis can cause respiratory symptoms, it would not explain the diffuse infiltrates seen on the chest X-ray or the severe hypoxemia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Pulmonary fibrosis: Pulmonary fibrosis is a chronic lung condition characterized by the formation of scar tissue in the lungs, leading to progressive and irreversible lung damage. It typically presents with a gradual onset of symptoms, such as shortness of breath and cough, and the chest X-ray findings would show a reticular or honeycomb pattern. The acute onset, severe hypoxemia, and diffuse infiltrates seen in the chest X-ray are not consistent with pulmonary fibrosis"}],"qi":[10363]},{"n":69,"q":"A 55-year-old patient presented with chronic projectile vomiting and weight loss. What is the expected metabolic abnormality to be seen here?","o":[{"t":"Hypokalemic hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis with hyponatremia"},{"t":"Hypokalemic hypochloremic metabolic acidosis with hypernatremia"},{"t":"Hypokalemic hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis with hypercalcemia"},{"t":"Hypokalemic hypochloremic metabolic acidosis with hyponatremia"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Hypokalemic hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis with hyponatremia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Based on the provided information of chronic projectile vomiting and weight loss in a 55-year-old patient, the expected metabolic abnormality is Hypokalemic hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis with hyponatremia. Chronic vomiting can lead to loss of gastric acid (hydrochloric acid) and chloride ions (hypochloremia). This loss of acid and chloride results in metabolic alkalosis. Additionally, prolonged vomiting can lead to loss of potassium (hypokalemia) through gastric secretions. Hyponatremia (low sodium levels) may occur as a result of fluid and electrolyte imbalances caused by vomiting."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":70,"q":"What should be the subsequent course of action in managing a patient aged 60, who is a diagnosed COPD patient, admitted to the ICU, and currently receiving 2 liters of oxygen therapy, but is observed to be hypoventilating with an arterial blood gas analysis revealing a pH level of 7.33 and pCO2 levels of 64 mmHg?","o":[{"t":"Non-invasive PPV should be given"},{"t":"Invasive PPV should be given"},{"t":"Increase the oxygen flow rate"},{"t":"Intubate and start on ventilator"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Non-invasive PPV should be given:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In patients with exacerbations of COPD who present with acute respiratory failure and hypoventilation, NIPPV is considered the initial treatment of choice. NIPPV delivers positive pressure ventilation via a mask or similar interface without the need for endotracheal intubation. It helps to improve ventilation, oxygenation, and alleviate respiratory distress by assisting the patient's breathing effort."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B & D - Invasive PPV should be given & Intubate and start on ventilator: Invasive positive pressure ventilation (intubation and starting on a ventilator) is generally reserved for cases where NIPPV fails or is contraindicated. It is associated with potential risks and complications, and therefore, NIPPV is preferred as the initial intervention in this scenario."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Increase the oxygen flow rate: Increasing the oxygen flow rate alone would not adequately address the underlying issue of hypoventilation and high pCO 2 levels. It may improve oxygenation temporarily, but it does not address the ventilation problem and the resulting respiratory acidosis."}]},{"n":71,"q":"A patient with no comorbidities presents with electrolyte values as shown below. The patient had a normal ECG. What is the preferred treatment for this patient among the options given below? Serum K+: 3.0mmol/L","o":[{"t":"Calcium gluconate"},{"t":"Sodium bicarbonate"},{"t":"Potklor"},{"t":"Insulin with dextrose"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Potklor:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypokalemia is when the amount of potassium in your blood is too low. Normal levels of potassium for an adult range from 3.5 to 5.2 mEq/L (3.5 to 5.2 mmol/L). Anything lower than 3 mEq/L (3 mmol/L) may be considered severe hypokalemia. The preferred treatment for a patient with a serum potassium level of 3.0 mmol/L and no comorbidities, along with a normal ECG, would be PotChlor (Oral potassium chloride). It can help restore potassium levels to a normal range and alleviate the symptoms associated with hypokalemia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Calcium gluconate: Calcium gluconate is not the preferred treatment for hypokalemia. Calcium gluconate is used to treat conditions such as hyperkalemia (high potassium levels) or to counteract the effects of calcium channel blocker overdose. It does not directly address low potassium levels."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Sodium bicarbonate: Sodium bicarbonate is not the preferred treatment for hypokalemia. Sodium bicarbonate is used to treat Metabolic acidosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Insulin with dextrose: Insulin with dextrose is used in the treatment of hyperkalemia, not hypokalemia. Insulin with dextrose helps shift potassium from the extracellular space into the cells, thereby lowering blood potassium levels. However, in this case, the patient's potassium level is low, so insulin with dextrose is not appropriate"}]},{"n":72,"q":"What is the most effective diagnostic test for a 50-year-old individual who is a chronic smoker and is experiencing mild, repeated episodes of coughing up blood without any accompanying symptoms of fever or weight loss? The chest x-ray shows no abnormalities, and the sputum test is negative for Acid-Fast Bacilli (AFB).","o":[{"t":"MRI"},{"t":"CT"},{"t":"Fiberoptic bronchoscopy"},{"t":"Bronchography"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - CT:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In the given scenario, a 50-year-old chronic smoker with mild recurrent hemoptysis and normal chest X-ray, CT scan (computed tomography) is the most useful diagnostic investigation. CT scan provides detailed imaging of the lungs and can help identify subtle abnormalities that may not be visible on a chest X-ray. It can detect small nodules, bronchial wall thickening, or other structural changes in the lungs that could be contributing to the hemoptysis. CT scan is commonly used in the evaluation of hemoptysis, especially when the chest X-ray is normal or inconclusive."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C, and D are incorrect. CT scan is a more appropriate, non invasive, diagnostic next step in the management of this patient."}]},{"n":73,"q":"Nasal polyps are commonly associated with which of the following?","o":[{"t":"Intrinsic asthma"},{"t":"Brittle asthma"},{"t":"Extrinsic asthma"},{"t":"Exercise - induced asthma"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Intrinsic asthma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nasal polyps are commonly associated with intrinsic asthma. Intrinsic asthma, also known as non-allergic or non-atopic asthma, is characterized by asthma symptoms that are not triggered by specific allergens. It can be caused by factors such as respiratory infections, irritants, exercise, cold air, stress, or certain medications. Nasal polyps are frequently seen in individuals with intrinsic asthma, suggesting a connection between these two conditions."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are incorrect and are not characteristically associated with nasal polyps."}]},{"n":74,"q":"A 30-year-old HIV positive man presents with fever for 3 weeks, dry cough an significant weight loss. His chest x-ray is given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Staphylococcal pneumonia"},{"t":"Pneumococcal pneumonia"},{"t":"Tuberculosis"},{"t":"Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PJP):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (formerly known as Pneumocystis carinii pneumonia) is a fungal infection that commonly affects individuals with weakened immune systems, such as those with HIV/AIDS. The presentation of fever, dry cough, significant weight loss, and chest X-ray findings of diffuse interstitial infiltrates and symmetrical perihilar involvement are highly suggestive of PJP ."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and C are incorrect."}],"qi":[10372]},{"n":75,"q":"On performing a pulmonary function test, reduction in FEV1/FVC is characteristic of:","o":[{"t":"Restrictive disease"},{"t":"Obstructive disease"},{"t":"Normal lung function"},{"t":"Interstitial lung disease"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Obstructive disease:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In obstructive lung diseases , the airways become narrowed or obstructed , making it d ifficult for air to flow out during expiration."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The reduction in the FEV1/FVC ratio is a characteristic finding in obstructive lung diseases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The FEV1 decreases more than the FVC , leading to a decreased FEV1/FVC ratio."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This reduction indicates impaired airflow during expiration, resulting in increased residual volume in the lungs."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Examples of obstructive lung diseases include asthma, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), and bronchiectasis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Restrictive disease:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In restrictive lung diseases, the expansion of lung tissue is limited, resulting in reduced lung volumes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Restrictive lung diseases typically do not cause a significant reduction in the FEV1/FVC ratio."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Instead, both FEV1 and FVC are proportionally reduced, maintaining a relatively normal FEV1/FVC ratio or even slightly increased."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Examples of restrictive lung diseases include pulmonary fibrosis, interstitial lung disease, and chest wall deformities."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Normal lung function:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Reduction in the FEV1/FVC ratio is not characteristic of normal lung function."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Interstitial lung disease:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Interstitial lung disease is a form of restrictive lung disease. FEV1/FVC might be normal or slightly elevated in these patients."}]},{"n":76,"q":"Which of the subsequent options is not typically observed in allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis?","o":[{"t":"Low serum IgE levels"},{"t":"Cough"},{"t":"Wheezing"},{"t":"Central bronchiectasis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Low serum IgE levels:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Low serum IgE levels are incorrect. Elevated levels of serum IgE are a characteristic feature of allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis. IgE-mediated hypersensitivity to Aspergillus antigens leads to the production of specific IgE antibodies, resulting in an increased serum IgE level."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Cough: Cough is a common symptom of allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis. It is typically productive and may be associated with the production of brownish sputum plugs."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Wheezing: Wheezing is a common finding in allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis. It is caused by the airway inflammation and narrowing resulting from the immune response to Aspergillus antigens."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Central bronchiectasis: Centeal bronchiectasis is a common feature of allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (ABPA). It is characterized by the dilation and thickening of the airways in the distal portions of the lungs."}]},{"n":77,"q":"Which of the following options best represents 'very severe COPD' according to the GOLD criteria?","o":[{"t":"FEV1/FVC <0.7 and FEV1<30%"},{"t":"FEV1/FVC <0.7 and FEV1<70%"},{"t":"FEV1/FVC <0.7 and FEV1<50%"},{"t":"Both a and c"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - FEV1/FVC <0.7 and FEV1<30%:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The Gold's criteria for very severe COPD is: FEV1/FVC <0.7 and FEV1 <30%"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"GOLD Classification for COPD:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mild COPD FEV1/ FVC<0.7 FEV1‡80%"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Moderate COPD FEV1/ FVC <0.7 FEV1 = 50-80%"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Severe COPD FEV1/ FVC <0.7 FEV1 = 30-50%"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Very sever COPD FEV1/ FVC <0.7 FEV1 <30%"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":78,"q":"What is the cause of infertility in individuals with Kartagener's syndrome?","o":[{"t":"Oligospermia"},{"t":"Asthenospermia"},{"t":"Undescended testes"},{"t":"Epididymis obstruction"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Asthenospermia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Kartagener's syndrome, also known as primary ciliary dyskinesia (PCD), is a rare genetic disorder that affects the structure and function of cilia. Cilia are hair-like structures present on the surface of cells, including cells in the respiratory tract and reproductive organs. In Kartagener's syndrome, there is a defect in the structure and/or function of cilia, leading to various clinical manifestations, including respiratory problems and infertility."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In the context of infertility in Kartagener's syndrome, the most likely cause is asthenospermia, which refers to reduced or absent sperm motility. Cilia play a crucial role in sperm motility, as they provide the propulsive force needed for sperm movement within the female reproductive tract. In Kartagener's syndrome, the defective ciliary function can result in impaired sperm motility, leading to reduced fertility."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are not specific features of Kartagener's syndrome and are incorrect."}]},{"n":79,"q":"Among the following choices, which organism is the most probable cause of ventilator-associated pneumonia?","o":[{"t":"Clostridium"},{"t":"Moraxella catarrhalis"},{"t":"Acinetobacter"},{"t":"Hemophilus"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Acinetobacter:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP) is a type of pneumonia that occurs in patients who are on mechanical ventilation. It is typically acquired during the hospital stay and is often caused by bacterial pathogens. Among the options provided, Acinetobacter is the most likely causative organism of VAP."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Acinetobacter is a gram-negative bacterium that is known for its ability to survive on environmental surfaces and medical equipment. It can colonize the respiratory tract of ventilated patients and has the potential to cause serious infections, including pneumonia. Acinetobacter infections are often associated with healthcare settings, especially intensive care units (ICUs)."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Option A Clostridium species are anaerobic bacteria commonly associated with gastrointestinal infections and gas gangrene, not typically implicated in ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Option B Moraxella catarrhalis is another bacterium that is more commonly associated with community-acquired respiratory tract infections, such as otitis media and exacerbations of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), rather than VAP"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Option D Haemophilus species, such as Haemophilus influenzae, are more commonly associated with upper respiratory tract infections and community-acquired pneumonia, rather than VAP."}]},{"n":80,"q":"Which of the following drugs is commonly used for treating community-acquired pneumonia in the outpatient setting?","o":[{"t":"Vancomycin"},{"t":"Ceftriaxone"},{"t":"Azithromycin"},{"t":"Streptomycin"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Azithromycin:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Community-acquired pneumonia (CAP) refers to pneumonia that is acquired outside of healthcare facilities. In the outpatient setting, the most common pathogens causing CAP are typically atypical bacteria, such as Mycoplasma pneumoniae and Chlamydophila pneumoniae, as well as certain respiratory viruses. Azithromycin, a macrolide antibiotic, is commonly used as an empiric treatment option for CAP in the outpatient setting."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Azithromycin has several advantages that make it suitable for outpatient management of CAP. It has excellent activity against atypical pathogens, including Mycoplasma pneumoniae and Chlamydophila pneumoniae. It also has a long half-life, allowing for once-daily dosing, which enhances patient compliance."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Vancomycin: Vancomycin is an antibiotic primarily used for the treatment of Gram-positive bacterial infections, particularly those caused by methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA). It is not typically used as an initial empiric treatment for community-acquired pneumonia in the outpatient setting."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Ceftriaxone: Ceftriaxone is a broad-spectrum cephalosporin antibiotic commonly used for the treatment of various bacterial infections. While it may be used for the treatment of CAP in specific cases, such as when there is a concern for more resistant pathogens or in patients with specific risk factors, it is not the first-line treatment choice in most cases of outpatient CAP."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Streptomycin: Streptomycin is an aminoglycoside antibiotic that has activity against certain Gram-negative bacteria. However, it is not commonly used as a first-line treatment for community-acquired pneumonia, especially in the outpatient setting."}]},{"n":81,"q":"A 45-year patient working in a factory for the past 20 years presents with breathlessness. HRCT chest shows pleural thickening and fibrosis. Diagnosis is?","o":[{"t":"Asbestosis"},{"t":"Coal worker pneumoconiosis"},{"t":"Silicosis"},{"t":"Berylliosis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Asbestosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Asbestosis is a lung disease caused by long-term exposure to asbestos fibers . In this condition, asbestos fibers cause inflammation and scarring of the lung tissue, leading to breathing difficulties and other respiratory symptoms."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The given clinical scenario of a 45-year-old patient with a history of working in a factory for 20 years and presenting with breathlessness is suggestive of an occupational lung disease. The HRCT chest findings of pleural thickening and fibrosis further support the diagnosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Coal worker pneumoconiosis: This is a lung disease caused by inhalation of coal dust. It is characterized by the deposition of coal dust particles in the lungs, leading to inflammation and scarring. However, the presentation in the given scenario is not consistent with coal worker pneumoconiosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Silicosis: Silicosis is a lung disease caused by inhalation of crystalline silica particles. It commonly affects individuals working in industries such as mining, construction, and sandblasting. The characteristic HRCT findings in silicosis include multiple small nodules in the upper lung zones. Pleural thickening and fibrosis are not typical features of silicosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Berylliosis: Berylliosis is a lung disease caused by exposure to beryllium, commonly found in certain industries such as aerospace, electronics, and dental laboratories. It can lead to granulomatous inflammation in the lungs. The clinical presentation and HRCT findings described in the scenario are not consistent with berylliosis."}]},{"n":82,"q":"Which of the following is a causative agent of Farmer's Lung?","o":[{"t":"Thermophilic Actinomycetes"},{"t":"Aspergillus Fumigatus"},{"t":"Actinobacter"},{"t":"Aspergillus Flavus"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Thermophilic Actinomycetes:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Farmer's Lung, also known as hypersensitivity pneumonitis, is an allergic lung disease caused by the inhalation of various organic dust particles. Thermophilic Actinomycetes, which are bacteria-like microorganisms commonly found in moldy hay, straw, and grain, are one of the causative agents of Farmer's Lung. When individuals sensitized to these microorganisms inhale the dust containing them, an immune response is triggered, leading to inflammation and lung damage."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are not associated with Farmer's lung."}]},{"n":83,"q":"Which of the following is associated with metastasis to the brain?","o":[{"t":"Lung cancer"},{"t":"Hepatocellular carcinoma"},{"t":"Prostate cancer"},{"t":"Breast cancer"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Lung cancer:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lung cancer is the most common primary cancer that metastasizes to the brain . The lungs are a common site for the development of cancerous cells, and when these cells spread to other parts of the body, including the brain, they can form brain metastases."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":84,"q":"Which of the following is false about Mycoplasma pneumoniae?","o":[{"t":"Responds well to amoxiclav"},{"t":"Antibodies are useful in diagnosis"},{"t":"Chest X-ray shows bilateral infiltrates"},{"t":"Can be cultured in a cell-free medium"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Responds well to amoxiclav:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mycoplasma pneumonia is caused by the bacterium Mycoplasma pneumoniae, and it is typically treated with antibiotics such as macrolides (e.g., azithromycin) or tetracyclines (e.g., doxycycline). Amoxiclav, which is a combination of amoxicillin and clavulanic acid, is not considered a first-line treatment for Mycoplasma pneumonia. Therefore, the statement that mycoplasma pneumonia responds well to amoxiclav is false."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Antibodies are useful in diagnosis: Antibodies are indeed useful in the diagnosis of Mycoplasma pneumonia. Serologic testing, such as the detection of specific antibodies (IgM and IgG) against Mycoplasma pneumoniae, can be helpful in confirming the diagnosis. These antibodies can be detected using tests like enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) or complement fixation tests."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Chest X-ray shows bilateral infiltrates: Chest X-ray findings in Mycoplasma pneumonia can vary, but bilateral infiltrates are commonly seen. Infiltrates may appear as patchy or diffuse opacities in both lungs. However, it is important to note that chest X-ray findings alone cannot definitively diagnose Mycoplasma pneumonia and should be correlated with clinical symptoms and other diagnostic tests."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Can be cultured in a cell-free medium: Mycoplasma pneumoniae is a unique bacterium that lacks a cell wall and requires a specialized cell-free medium for culture. It cannot be grown on conventional culture media. The absence of a cell wall makes it resistant to certain antibiotics that target cell wall synthesis, such as penicillins and cephalosporins."}]},{"n":85,"q":"In the given acid-base nomogram diagram, areas marked A and D indicated which of the following conditions?","o":[{"t":"Chronic respiratory acidosis, acute metabolic alkalosis"},{"t":"Acute metabolic alkalosis, acute respiratory acidosis"},{"t":"Acute respiratory acidosis, acute respiratory alkalosis"},{"t":"Acute respiratory acidosis, acute metabolic alkalosis"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Acute respiratory acidosis, acute respiratory alkalosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A - Acute respiratory acidosis: Acute respiratory acidosis occurs when there is an increase in the partial pressure of carbon dioxide (PaCO 2 ) in the blood due to inadequate ventilation. This can be caused by conditions such as pneumonia, pulmonary edema, or airway obstruction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"D - Acute respiratory alkalosis: Acute respiratory alkalosis occurs when there is a decrease in PaCO 2 in the blood due to excessive ventilation. It can be caused by hyperventilation due to anxiety, fever, or high altitude."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and D are incorrect."}],"qi":[10382]},{"n":86,"q":"What is the most common pulmonary manifestation of SLE?","o":[{"t":"Shrinking lung syndrome"},{"t":"Pleuritis"},{"t":"Intra alveolar hemorrhage"},{"t":"Interstitial inflammation"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Pleuritis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pleuritis, inflammation of the pleura (the membranes lining the lungs and chest cavity), is the most common pulmonary manifestation of SLE. It can cause chest pain, pleural effusion (accumulation of fluid in the pleural space), and a characteristic friction rub on auscultation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Shrinking lung syndrome: Shrinking lung syndrome is a rare complication of systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) characterized by progressive dyspnea and restrictive lung function. However, it is not the most common pulmonary manifestation of SLE."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Intra-alveolar hemorrhage: Intra-alveolar hemorrhage refers to bleeding into the air sacs of the lungs. While it can occur in SLE, it is less common compared to pleuritis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Interstitial inflammation: Interstitial inflammation refers to inflammation of the lung interstitium, which is the tissue between the air sacs. While interstitial lung disease can occur in SLE, it is not the most common pulmonary manifestation."}]},{"n":87,"q":"What is the most common presentation of inhalational anthrax?","o":[{"t":"Atypical pneumonia"},{"t":"Haemorrhagic mediastinitis"},{"t":"Lung abscess"},{"t":"Broncho-pulmonary pneumonia"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Haemorrhagic mediastinitis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This is the most common presentation of inhalational anthrax. It is characterized by severe chest pain, shortness of breath, and mediastinal widening on imaging. Hemorrhagic mediastinitis refers to the presence of bleeding and inflammation in the mediastinum, the area between the lungs."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Atypical pneumonia: This is not the most common presentation of inhalational anthrax. While respiratory symptoms can occur, they are not typically indicative of a typical pneumonia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Lung abscess: Although lung abscess can occur in some cases of inhalational anthrax, it is not the most common presentation. Lung abscesses are localized collections of pus within the lung parenchyma."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Broncho-pulmonary pneumonia: While pneumonia can occur in inhalational anthrax, it typically presents as a severe form with features of haemorrhagic mediastinitis rather than a typical broncho-pulmonary pneumonia."}]},{"n":88,"q":"A 30-year-old man presented the findings shown in the given image. What is this physical finding called?","o":[{"t":"Spoon-shaped nails"},{"t":"Raynaud's phenomenon"},{"t":"Pallor"},{"t":"Clubbing"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Clubbing:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The above image is that of clubbing, which is a selective bulbous enlargement of the distal segments of the fingers and toes due to the proliferation of connective tissues."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and C are incorrect."}],"qi":[10388]},{"n":89,"q":"Which of the following agents is most likely causing the condition in a 60-year-old woman, who had a kidney transplant 2 months ago, and is now experiencing a high fever, severe difficulty in breathing, and a dry cough with a hacking sound? Chest x-ray shows widespread interstitial infiltrates in both lungs, and urine examination reveals the presence of cells with an 'owl-eye' appearance.","o":[{"t":"Adenovirus"},{"t":"Cytomegalovirus"},{"t":"Influenza virus"},{"t":"Respiratory syncytial"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Cytomegalovirus:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In the above case, owl’s eye intranuclear inclusion bodies are seen in cytomegalovirus infection. CMV infection is a significant cause of mortality after an allogeneic transplant, especially in renal transplant patients."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":90,"q":"Which of the following features are true about Allergic Broncho Pulmonary Aspergillosis? Distal bronchiectasis Serum precipitins to Aspergillus Increased lgE Levels Seen in asthmatics","o":[{"t":"1,2,3 only"},{"t":"2,3,4 only"},{"t":"1,3,4 only"},{"t":"All of the above"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - 2,3,4 only:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Distal bronchiectasis: This feature is not typically associated with Allergic Broncho Pulmonary Aspergilloma (ABPA). Distal bronchiectasis is more commonly seen in other conditions such as cystic fibrosis. In ABPA, Central Bronchiectasis is seen."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum precipitins to Aspergillus: This is a characteristic feature of ABPA. Individuals with ABPA often have detectable serum precipitins (specific antibodies) against Aspergillus."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased IgE levels: ABPA is characterized by an elevated level of IgE (immunoglobulin E) in the blood. This is a result of the allergic response to Aspergillus antigens."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Seen in asthmatics: ABPA is frequently associated with asthma. It is estimated that about 10% of individuals with asthma may develop ABPA."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":91,"q":"Why is COVID-19 a major global concern despite influenza spreading faster than COVID-19?","o":[{"t":"Median incubation period is more in influenza"},{"t":"Reproductive number for COVID-19 is 2-2.5"},{"t":"Serial interval is 5-6 days in influenza"},{"t":"Reproductive number for Influenza is higher"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Reproductive number for COVID-19 is 2-2.5:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The reproductive number (R0) represents the average number of people that an infected individual will infect. The estimated R0 for COVID-19 is around 2-2.5, which means that each infected person, on average, can transmit the virus to 2-2.5 others. This indicates the potential for sustained transmission and makes COVID-19 highly contagious."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Median incubation period is more in influenza: The median incubation period of COVID-19 is generally longer compared to influenza. Influenza typically has a shorter incubation period, ranging from 1-4 days, while COVID-19 has a median incubation period of around 5-6 days."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Serial interval is 5-6 days in influenza: The serial interval refers to the time between the onset of symptoms in one infected individual and the onset of symptoms in the secondary infected individual. Influenza typically has a shorter serial interval, usually around 2-3 days, indicating a relatively rapid spread of the virus from person to person."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Reproductive number for Influenza is more: The reproductive number for influenza is generally lower compared to COVID-19. While the exact R0 for different influenza strains can vary, it is typically lower than the estimated R0 for COVID-19. This means that COVID-19 has a higher potential for transmission within a susceptible population compared to influenza."}]},{"n":92,"q":"In good pasture syndrome, which organ is involved apart from lung?","o":[{"t":"Kidney"},{"t":"Liver"},{"t":"Spleen"},{"t":"Heart"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Kidney:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Goodpasture syndrome is an autoimmune disease characterized by the presence of autoantibodies that target the basement membrane in the lungs and kidneys. These autoantibodies primarily bind to the type IV collagen present in the alveolar basement membrane of the lungs and the glomerular basement membrane of the kidneys."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The binding of autoantibodies to the glomerular basement membrane in the kidneys leads to the activation of an immune response and subsequent inflammation. This inflammatory response can cause damage to the kidney tissue and impair its function."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":93,"q":"What is the clinical diagnosis for a patient who experiences sudden and severe respiratory distress after undergoing internal jugular vein catheterization?","o":[{"t":"Pneumothorax"},{"t":"Sepsis"},{"t":"ARDS"},{"t":"Infective endarteritis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Pneumothorax:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The sudden-onset severe respiratory distress following the placement of an internal jugular vein catheter is highly suggestive of pneumothorax, which is the presence of air in the pleural space surrounding the lungs. When air accumulates in the pleural space, it can cause the lung to collapse partially or completely, leading to respiratory distress, chest pain, and other symptoms."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":94,"q":"Given below is an electron microscopic image of the lung tissue. A patient infected with SARS-CoV2 presents with ARDS. The infection would affect and expand which of the labeled layers?","o":[{"t":"B"},{"t":"A"},{"t":"D"},{"t":"None of the above"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - A:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In the electron microscopic image of the lung tissue, the layer labeled \"A\" represents the endothelium."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"SARS-CoV-2, the virus causing COVID-19, primarily affects the respiratory system and can lead to acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In ARDS, the virus can directly invade the endothelial cells lining the blood vessels in the lungs."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The viral invasion of endothelial cells can disrupt their normal function and integrity, leading to increased vascular permeability, inflammation, and the development of pulmonary oedema."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - B:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In the electron microscopic image of the lung tissue, the layer labeled \"B\" represents the basal laminae."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The basal laminae provide structural support to the epithelial cells and separate them from the underlying connective tissue."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"While basal laminae may be involved in certain lung diseases, they are not the primary site of infection in SARS-CoV-2-related ARDS."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - D:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In the electron microscopic image of the lung tissue, the layer labeled \"D\" represents the epithelium."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The epithelium lines the airways and is involved in the defense mechanisms of the respiratory system."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"While it can be affected by SARS-CoV-2, the primary target and primary pathological changes occur in the endothelium."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - None of the above:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This option is incorrect."}],"qi":[10395]},{"n":95,"q":"A male patient presents to the emergency department with features of increased ICP and CO2 narcosis. The arterial blood gas report is as follows: pH- 7.2; pCO2- 81 mmHg; and HCO3- 40meq/L. which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Respiratory alkalosis"},{"t":"Metabolic acidosis"},{"t":"Respiratory acidosis"},{"t":"Metabolic alkalosis"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Respiratory acidosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In respiratory acidosis, once CO 2 spikes, there is excess production of hydrogen ions, leading to a fall in pH."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"There is a compensatory increases in bicarbonate values to neuralize the values of hydrogen by the reabsorption from the kidney."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This pattern of results is most usually seen in patients accompanying hypoventilation , which leads to an increase in carbon dioxide retention and a subsequent decrease in pH."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Respiratory causes leading ot hypoventilation include Obstruction, asthma, COPD exacerbation, severe ARDS, barotrauma, Polio, Muscular dystrophy."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Respiratory alkalosis: There is CO 2 washout, which causes hyperventilation and decrease hydrogen ions and bicarbonate loss from PCT as compensation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Metabolic acidosis: This condition results from a decreased pH and decreased bicarbonate levels."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Metabolic alkalosis: This condition results from a raised pH and high bicarbonate levels."}]},{"n":96,"q":"The arterial blood gas findings of a patient are pH = 7.12, pCO2 = 50, HCO3 = 28. What is the diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Respiratory acidosis with metabolic compensation"},{"t":"Metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation"},{"t":"Respiratory alkalosis with metabolic compensation"},{"t":"Metabolic alkalosis with respiratory compensation"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Respiratory acidosis with metabolic compensation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"pH = 7.12: This value indicates acidemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"pCO 2 = 50: An elevated pCO 2 indicates respiratory acidosis, as it suggests inadequate elimination of carbon dioxide by the lungs."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"HCO 3 = 28: The HCO 3 level is within the normal range, suggesting metabolic compensation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Based on these findings, the diagnosis is respiratory acidosis with metabolic compensation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In respiratory acidosis, once the CO 2 spikes, there is excess production of H + ions leading to a fall in pH (acidosis). There is a compensatory increase in bicarbonate values to neutralize the values of hydrogen by the reabsorption from the kidney."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation: Metabolic acidosis is characterized by a low pH and low bicarbonate level."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Respiratory alkalosis with metabolic compensation: Respiratory alkalosis is characterized by a high pH and low pCO 2 ."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Metabolic alkalosis with respiratory compensation: Metabolic alkalosis is characterized by a high pH and high bicarbonate level."}]},{"n":97,"q":"A young man presents with a sudden onset of severe headache, dizziness, breathlessness, and blurring of vision while trekking on a mountain at an altitude of 5000 meters. He is unable to climb any further. Which of the following develops at high-altitude?","o":[{"t":"Metabolic alkalosis"},{"t":"Respiratory alkalosis"},{"t":"Respiratory acidosis"},{"t":"Metabolic acidosis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Respiratory alkalosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In respiratory alkalosis, there is carbon dioxide washout, which causes hyperventilation and decreases hydrogen ions. To compensate for this, bicarbonate loss occurs from the kidney."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Causes of respiratory alkalosis CNS- Pain, anxiety, hysteria, fever, CVA CHF, pulmonary edema High altitude pulmonary edema Pulmonary embolism Flail chest Sepsis, heat exposure"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CNS- Pain, anxiety, hysteria, fever, CVA"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CHF, pulmonary edema"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"High altitude pulmonary edema"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pulmonary embolism"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Flail chest"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sepsis, heat exposure"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CNS- Pain, anxiety, hysteria, fever, CVA"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CHF, pulmonary edema"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"High altitude pulmonary edema"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pulmonary embolism"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Flail chest"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sepsis, heat exposure"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Metabolic alkalosis- Metabolic alkalosis is caused by vomitting, diarrhea, drugs like diuretics."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Respiratory acidosis - Respiratory acidosis is caused by CVA, Asthama, ARDS, COPD exacerbation, Barotrauma."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Metabolic acidosis- Metabolic acidosis is caused by Ketoacidosis, Uremia, Lactic acidosis, toxins"}]},{"n":98,"q":"A smoker patient has yellow copious foul-smelling sputum that increases significantly on postural change and had this problem for a few years. The first differential diagnosis is?","o":[{"t":"Asthma"},{"t":"Pneumonia"},{"t":"Bronchiectasis"},{"t":"Cancer"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Bronchiectasis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bronchiectasis is a condition characterized by destruction and dilatation of proximal & medium-sized bronchi (>2 mm)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Airways are filled with pus & result in the features of bronchorrhea."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mucopurulent sputum and bronchorrhea are the characteristic features."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other features are: Dyspnea on exertion Crept > rhonchi present Clubbing Weakness, weight loss Cyanosis may or may not be present. Cor pulmonale"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dyspnea on exertion"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Crept > rhonchi present"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clubbing"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Weakness, weight loss"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cyanosis may or may not be present."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cor pulmonale"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dyspnea on exertion"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Crept > rhonchi present"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clubbing"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Weakness, weight loss"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cyanosis may or may not be present."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cor pulmonale"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Asthma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The hallmark feature of bronchial asthma is airway hyperresponsiveness leading to bronchospasm"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Pneumonia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Patients with pneumonia present with fever, tachypnea, cough with rusty sputum."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Cancer"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lung cancer presents with chest pain, fatigue, breathlessness, weight loss, etc.,"}]},{"n":99,"q":"Which of the following is a derived / calculated value in Arterial Blood Gas analysis ?","o":[{"t":"pH"},{"t":"pO 2"},{"t":"pCO 2"},{"t":"Bicarbonate"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Bicarbonate:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bicarbonate concentration is calculated or derived from PaCO 2 and pH using the Henderson-Hasselbach equation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"pCO 2 + H 2 O= H 2 CO 3 n H + + HCO 3 -"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"An increase in HCO 3 - occurs with either metabolic alkalosis or respiratory acidosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A decrease in HCO 3 - occurs in metabolic acidosis or respiratory alkalosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A, B, C:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These values are measured values in the ABG analysis."}]},{"n":100,"q":"Criterion that differentiates exudative from transudative pleural effusion?","o":[{"t":"Low protein in pleural fluid"},{"t":"High protein in pleural fluid"},{"t":"Low sugar in pleural fluid"},{"t":"High sugar in pleural fluid."}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - High protein in pleural fluid:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Transudative pleural effusion occurs due to the imbalance between the hydrostatic and oncotic pressure that causes an increase in pleural fluid in interstitial space."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"An exudative pleural effusion occurs due to inflammation or infections. E.g. bacterial pneumonia, malignancy, viral infection."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Light’s Criteria is used to differentiate transudative and exudative pleural effusion/hydrothorax."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"High protein in the pleural fluid indicates it is an exudative type of pleural effusion."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Light's Criteria Pleural fluid protein/serum protein > 0.5fiexudate – Fibrin Elevated. Pleural fluid LDH/ Serum LDH >0.6 fiexudate – Fibrin Elevated. Pleural fluid LDH >2/3 rd of the upper limit in the serum “At least one out of the above conditions is fulfilled for exudate.” Misidentifies 25% of transudate as exudate."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pleural fluid protein/serum protein > 0.5fiexudate – Fibrin Elevated."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pleural fluid LDH/ Serum LDH >0.6 fiexudate – Fibrin Elevated."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pleural fluid LDH >2/3 rd of the upper limit in the serum"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"“At least one out of the above conditions is fulfilled for exudate.”"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Misidentifies 25% of transudate as exudate."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pleural fluid protein/serum protein > 0.5fiexudate – Fibrin Elevated."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pleural fluid LDH/ Serum LDH >0.6 fiexudate – Fibrin Elevated."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pleural fluid LDH >2/3 rd of the upper limit in the serum"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"“At least one out of the above conditions is fulfilled for exudate.”"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Misidentifies 25% of transudate as exudate."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Low protein in pleural fluid:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Low protein in pleural fluidice indicates transudative type of pleural effusion."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Low sugar in pleural fluid:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sugar estimation is not used to differentiate between exudative and tansudative type of pleural effusion."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - High sugar in pleural fluid:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sugar estimation is not used to differentiate between exudative and tansudative type of pleural effusion."}]},{"n":101,"q":"A male patient with infertility presents a disorder comprising the triad of situs inversus, chronic sinusitis, and bronchiectasis. Which of the following is the correct diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Kartageners syndrome"},{"t":"Cystic fibrosis"},{"t":"Mucovicidosis"},{"t":"Klinefelter syndrome"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Kartagener syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Kartagener syndrome is a rare ciliary motility disorder mainly affecting the cilia of the respiratory tract and fallopian tube."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is an autosomal recessive disorder and occurs due to a defect in the dynein protein."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Patients usually present with otitis media, chronic recurrent rhinosinusitis, pneumonia, and bronchiectasis caused by pseudomonal infection."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The clinical traid of Kartagener syndrome is Situs inversus Chronic sinusitis Bronchiectasis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Situs inversus"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chronic sinusitis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bronchiectasis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other features include telecanthus, infertility in males, and sub-infertility in females"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Situs inversus"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chronic sinusitis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bronchiectasis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Cystic fibrosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cystic fibrosis presents with a productive cough, steatorrhea, and failure to thrive."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Mucovicidosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cystic fibrosis is also known as Mucovicidosis as the body secretions are very thick contributing to various manifestations."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Klinefelter syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Klinefelter syndrome is due to the presence of an extra X chromosome in males that present with tall stature, small testes, gynecomastia, and azoospermia."}]},{"n":102,"q":"Which of the following conditions will have an FEV1/FVC ratio < 0.7 and FEV1 < 80% of the predicted value along with wheezing in the morning hours and shortness of breath?","o":[{"t":"Asthma"},{"t":"ILD"},{"t":"IPF"},{"t":"Silicosis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Asthma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"FEV 1 of < 80%, FEV 1 /FVC ratio < 0.7, and decreased peak expiratory flow rate indicate mild asthma. This is demonstrated using spirometry."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Reversibility of airflow obstruction is demonstrated with an increase in FEV 1 ‡12% after 15 mins of inhaling salbutamol."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Severity of the disease FEV 1"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Mild asthma 70-80%"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Moderate asthma 40-69%"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Severe asthma <40%"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Respiratory arrest imminent <25%"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - ILD:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"FEV 1 is reduced but a reduced FEV 1 /FVC ratio is unusual in many forms of ILD."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - IPF:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"FEV 1 /FVC ratio is artifactually normal, as in this condition FVC value is reduced by raising RV."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Silicosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"FEV 1 decreases progressively over time in silicosis."}]},{"n":103,"q":"The below chest x-ray shows right sided white-out lung and contralateral tracheal deviation. In which of the following conditions is this seen?","o":[{"t":"Pneumothorax"},{"t":"Consolidation"},{"t":"Collapse"},{"t":"Thoracic mass"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct options"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Pleural effusion according to scenario - 1"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this X-ray, the trachea has deviated to the opposite side indicating a thoracic mass or pleural effusion."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Pneumothorax:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In pneumothorax, the lung fields are hyperluscent with absent vascular markings."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Consolidation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In Consolidation, haziness is present on X-ray but a complete white-out lung is not likely observed."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Collapse:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this X-ray, the trachea is pulled towards the same side of the white-out lung which indicates the collapse of the lung."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is also noticed in pneumonectomy and pulmonary fibrosis."}],"qi":[10400]},{"n":104,"q":"Kartagener syndrome is associated with defects in which of the following structures?","o":[{"t":"Microvilli"},{"t":"Microtubules"},{"t":"Cilia"},{"t":"Microfilaments"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Cilia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Kartagener Syndrome, also known as Primary Ciliary Dyskinesia (PCD), is an autosomal recessive disorder that affects the cilia. It is characterized by chronic productive cough and recurrent infections of upper and lower respiratory tract."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option C for an explanation."}]}]);
